1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:03,200
Today, I’m chatting with three of my AI researcher friends

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:08,960
from whom I learn a lot every time we talk. They also happen to be at somewhat open-ish

3
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:12,720
labs and companies, so you guys can actually say things on the record.

4
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,640
I’m joined by Beren Millidge, who is the CTO of Zyphra, which is developing open source models.

5
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:20,720
John Schulman is the chief scientist at Thinking Machines, previously a co-founder of

6
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:27,120
OpenAI, and led the RLHF work that led to ChatGPT. And Charlie O’Neill is head of

7
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:30,640
model training at Baseten. The first question I have:

8
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:38,080
If we’re in 2036 and we don’t have billions of crazy superintelligences running around that have

9
00:00:38,080 --> 00:00:43,760
radically transformed the world, what is the most likely reason that doesn’t end up being the case?

10
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:49,200
Other than exogenous political shocks, or there’s a war, or they ban AI or something.

11
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:55,360
What is the most likely technical reason that 2036 isn’t a crazy alien superintelligence world?

12
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:06,000
There’s been a classic thing, almost like Moravec’s paradox, where we think of the AI as,

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,040
"If it can do this, it’s going to be amazing." If it can solve these hard maths problems,

14
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:13,280
if it can win at chess, blah, blah, blah… Then it solves these things, and it’s not that impactful.

15
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:15,840
Obviously, it’s somewhat impactful, but not everything.

16
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:21,120
If somehow that continues, and there’s never the true spark of generalization that occurs,

17
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:26,080
I think that could lead to the AI just being extremely good at everything that people put

18
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:30,080
into a benchmark or put into an environment. But there’s still some persistent sim-to-real gap

19
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:33,760
which is somehow blocking everything. I think this is unlikely.

20
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,360
We do actually see this kind of generalization even from RL in practice already.

21
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:42,560
But if it is just ridiculously hard to generalize meta-learning, plus we don’t solve continual

22
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:48,000
learning and it’s just super hard and impossible… This would be my default scenario in that case.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,200
I agree with that. Humans have a lot of advantages over models now.

24
00:01:54,080 --> 00:02:00,800
Each time a new model comes out, it’ll catch up in some of these areas.

25
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:06,160
But you end up getting bottlenecked by the places where the model is weaker and where

26
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:11,600
it has worse judgment, or the models can’t check themselves well enough.

27
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:17,120
There’s this cycle that keeps repeating where a new model comes out and people are

28
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:21,840
blown away and they’re like, "This is it. This is AGI." But then they use it a bit,

29
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:27,360
and it starts to feel dumb after a month or so. That cycle just might keep going.

30
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:30,000
It’s hard to predict how many times it’s going to repeat.

31
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:35,760
Right now, you don’t get explosive growth in capabilities because

32
00:02:36,480 --> 00:02:39,440
you still get bottlenecked enough when you’re trying to do research and engineering.

33
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:46,400
Even if the model can write way more code than a person, it doesn’t make you 100X more productive.

34
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,480
So maybe there are just more of these cycles than we would expect.

35
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:58,240
For me, it’s a question of how far off the global optimum of "a learner you could have

36
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:03,680
on a chip" is from the transformer + RL, basically the current recipe.

37
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:12,320
People imagine that once you have an agent which is better than all humans at AI research,

38
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:17,360
even if it’s 0.1% better than all humans, then the fact that you can run hundreds of thousands, if

39
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:20,880
not millions, of these in parallel — and you can run them much faster as chips speed up — is going

40
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:25,680
to outweigh every other bottleneck. You’re eventually going to hit this very

41
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:29,920
fast takeoff with regards to self-improvement. I could imagine that if we continue along the

42
00:03:29,920 --> 00:03:35,040
trajectory that we’re currently on with that paradigm, where it’s basically self-attention,

43
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:41,600
RL, scaling up RL environments… Think about what happened with Moore’s law.

44
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:45,120
We had this very nice straight line and that held for a really, really long time.

45
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:50,000
But there were so many discrete discontinuities and innovations that had

46
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
to happen to keep that scaling law going. The same thing has happened with LLMs.

47
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,280
We had this pre-training scaling law, and then that was hitting diminishing returns.

48
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:02,880
Then we came up with RL and solved that, and then we got this new diminishing

49
00:04:02,880 --> 00:04:06,800
returns curve to hit that made it keep looking like a straight line going up.

50
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:11,200
So if it requires another one of those discontinuities to solve,

51
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:15,840
I’m not sure that the current method of training LLMs with these RL environments,

52
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:20,480
even RSI-targeted RL environments, would be able to discover that discontinuity.

53
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:24,880
If not, we’re probably going to hit this asymptotic curve.

54
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:31,280
But do you think the discontinuity will be harder than anything that’s come since 2012?

55
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:35,200
If we had the answer to that, we’d kind of have the ability to implement it.

56
00:04:35,200 --> 00:04:40,160
But maybe we should distinguish between a discontinuity which adds to the current paradigm,

57
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,840
which is cumulative — there’s something beyond the RL that we have to discover,

58
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:49,840
and maybe they’re capable of connecting the dots in that straight line — or, again,

59
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,880
how far off the global optimum are we? Do we have to go back and throw out

60
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:59,040
gradient descent and neural nets in general? I don’t think, if you continue to scale up the

61
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:02,240
current paradigm, an LLM, no matter how many LLMs you’re running, is necessarily capable

62
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:09,600
of discovering that if it’s too far away. The only hope really is if deep learning just

63
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:17,680
can’t get us to an AI which can at least dominate human research and human development,

64
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:20,800
including the human ability to come up with new paradigms and so forth.

65
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:26,240
Or, I don't know, maybe humans would also never have discovered the next learning architecture.

66
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:32,480
But to the extent humans could have discovered it eventually… But it just seems like… If you

67
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:36,480
just look at the progress that’s happened since 2012 till now, and you just continue

68
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:40,800
that on —I know it’s just been powered by huge amounts of compute scaling and so forth—

69
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:47,360
it would be weird if it just didn’t get to the point where it could dominate humans, at least

70
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:54,320
in R&D, especially over the next few years. Ryan Greenblatt was on the podcast recently.

71
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:55,280
He made this point that I’d be curious to get your thoughts on.

72
00:05:55,280 --> 00:06:00,080
You could imagine, as AIs get more and more capable, that they’re capable of making progress

73
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:07,040
on simulations which incentivize getting better at not only AI R&D, but at science generally.

74
00:06:07,040 --> 00:06:10,240
This is a thing that all the labs are targeting and many startups are targeting.

75
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:14,400
Another intuition pump is if you look at the Elo score of chess bots since the ’80s.

76
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:20,320
There’s a very linear increase in Elo over time. But there’s this huge discontinuity as they cross

77
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:25,920
the human range, from human experts always winning against AIs to human experts never winning against

78
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:31,920
AIs, as this linear increase in Elo happens. I agree with your point that so far, AI

79
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:36,480
capabilities have not been that big of a deal in terms of their end economic impact in the world.

80
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:39,680
But that is because they’re slowly rising in Elo relative to humans.

81
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,320
I agree it would be very surprising. The only way for this to not happen is if,

82
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:48,320
as you said, it somehow asymptotes just before. Because we’re already pretty close, in my opinion,

83
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:52,960
to where we’ll start crossing the human Elo score. So we’ll need to asymptote before that.

84
00:06:53,520 --> 00:06:55,840
That’s the only way — in this scenario you pose where somehow

85
00:06:55,840 --> 00:06:59,760
we’re sitting here in 2035 and everything is normal — for this to happen, I think.

86
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:03,040
The only other way is there’s some dramatic regulation on AI.

87
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:05,840
This is what I see as the most likely way for this scenario to happen,

88
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:10,080
actually, rather than a technical thing. I think there’s different kinds of research.

89
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:15,360
There’s research in the autoresearch style where the objective is already specified very cleanly

90
00:07:15,360 --> 00:07:18,960
and you’re optimizing that objective. I think everyone is picturing that if

91
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:22,560
we continue along this path of making pre-training loss go down and making our

92
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,440
environments have the reward on them go up, that’s going to lead to improvement.

93
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:29,440
But maybe what Ryan is talking about is this much more open-ended type of science which

94
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:33,760
is required for paradigm shifts, where we can’t specify the objective, and the AIs are definitely

95
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:37,520
not able to specify that objective either. We have to be really, really careful about how

96
00:07:37,520 --> 00:07:41,200
we specify objectives for any of these things. Maybe your point is that the nature of the

97
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:45,680
breakthroughs that have happened since 2012 is that we have found… In 2012,

98
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:49,120
people weren’t saying… I’m assuming, I don’t know, you guys were there.

99
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:51,040
Or at least John, you were there. But I was not.

100
00:07:51,040 --> 00:07:55,520
I was in primary school. Actually, John, I’m curious

101
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:01,920
for your wisdom of the ages, or wisdom of being in the trenches way back when.

102
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:06,160
Presumably, a big breakthrough was realizing that next token prediction is the… You wouldn’t

103
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:12,880
have thought that the nanoGPT speed run is the thing to be optimizing for in 2014.

104
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:16,640
But now that we have come to this new paradigm, you would think to do a speed run on that and

105
00:08:16,640 --> 00:08:20,960
have AIs get really good at that. But maybe there’s a next inner loop

106
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:25,760
to optimize that the AIs wouldn’t anticipate. There’s an outer loop of revenue or something

107
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:29,680
that eventually should be strong, but it’s a very slow outer loop.

108
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:39,840
In fact, I remember in the early OpenAI days having the intuition that just minimizing log

109
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:46,960
loss wasn’t going to get you to intelligence. Because the important bits are accounting for

110
00:08:46,960 --> 00:08:52,160
such a small fraction of the loss that it was going to be overwhelmed by noise.

111
00:08:52,160 --> 00:08:58,640
So just training a language model on next token prediction wasn’t going to learn the

112
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:02,880
interesting things you want it to learn. We needed to craft better objectives that

113
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:09,040
would put more emphasis on the important things. You can make all sorts of arguments for this.

114
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:17,120
You could say, "Oh, humans probably don’t learn how to model everything in our environment.

115
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:23,680
Most people can’t create a photorealistic reproduction of

116
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:30,800
some kind of scene they’ve looked at. So we must need a better objective."

117
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:35,120
But then it turned out that it just worked anyway. As you were pointing out, the inner loop,

118
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:39,680
even in current AI research, of post-training benchmarks or whatever, doesn’t necessarily

119
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:43,920
translate into what users like. Oh, yeah. The whole field relies

120
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:49,200
a lot on generalization and it’s very hard to predict when you’re going to get generalization,

121
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:52,640
or when you’re going to get some kind of out-of-distribution generalization.

122
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,680
We know that if you train on the task you care about, you’re going to do better.

123
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:06,640
But the most important advances are often types of generalization that we have no right to expect.

124
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:14,800
For example, from just pre-training on this very naive next-token-prediction objective

125
00:10:14,800 --> 00:10:24,000
to various tasks of interest that require understanding of the input in some deep way,

126
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:31,920
or learning some skill from pre-training that’s very rare and not heavily represented.

127
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:38,320
Then also generalization from these verifiable tasks to less verifiable ones,

128
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:43,040
this is also a type of generalization that there’s no reason a priori to expect.

129
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:48,720
This is an interesting question, because one intuition pump you could have for why you

130
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:55,200
would see some sort of singularity very rapidly — without even scaling up the inputs to AI progress

131
00:10:55,200 --> 00:11:02,400
that are not just AI labor — is that before every single 7-figure experiment you run, you spend an

132
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:08,240
equivalent amount of compute on AI labor. So you just have automated versions of you

133
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:15,200
guys spending a century thinking about what is the optimal experiment to run, doing small-scale

134
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:22,480
ablations, developing literally a century’s worth of theory, going back even before deep learning.

135
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:25,360
Before you decide what experiment to run, you’re doing extremely

136
00:11:25,360 --> 00:11:28,800
optimal setting up of the experiment. Then you do a century of thinking after the

137
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:33,360
experiment is over, where you’re analyzing what happened and what the next experiment to run is.

138
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:39,280
If you think hard enough, you probably could have expected some of these things beforehand.

139
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:44,480
There is probably some very clever way to do a small-scale experiment that’ll

140
00:11:44,480 --> 00:11:48,000
let you build the theory that then will generalize to the large-scale experiment.

141
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:52,240
So I would expect that we’re nowhere near the ceiling of how well you can do research.

142
00:11:53,920 --> 00:12:03,360
I would imagine a future where AI is doing a lot of analysis and theory building,

143
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:08,800
spending a comparable amount of compute to the amount that you’re spending on the experiments

144
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:15,440
themselves, doing various kinds of analysis and building a theory around what we’ve seen so far.

145
00:12:15,440 --> 00:12:19,280
I think there are really concrete examples of this when the objective is well specified.

146
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:24,160
All thinking can do is update your posterior based on the bits

147
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:28,880
that you’ve gotten since you formed your prior. You can’t gain any new bits from just thinking.

148
00:12:28,880 --> 00:12:32,240
But when the objective is well specified and there is this data sitting around,

149
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:35,040
I imagine there will be this big speed-up in the current paradigm we’re in.

150
00:12:35,040 --> 00:12:40,240
A good example of this is if you got an AI to think about the Kaplan scaling laws.

151
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:45,200
An AI at this point would have noticed, "Oh, they’ve just taken these intermediate

152
00:12:45,200 --> 00:12:49,040
checkpoints and didn’t account for the annealing, and so this is wrong."

153
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:54,240
That would have been caught years earlier. We would have cut off a year or two of

154
00:12:54,240 --> 00:12:59,680
progress just from that observation from an AI. Again, once the objective is well specified, which

155
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:04,160
is lower pre-training loss or whatever, there are many, many good examples where if you just thought

156
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:09,040
about it a bit more, you would have been able to cut down significantly on things that you’ve done.

157
00:13:09,040 --> 00:13:13,280
So muP, and how learning rate scales with model size, and realizing that

158
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:18,720
model width is important in that as well. I feel like you can really back out a lot of these

159
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:23,040
things and cut off a lot of low-hanging fruit. I would imagine a 10x speed-up if our thing is

160
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:27,600
just, "Maximize the objective we’re currently on." But I don’t see how that generalizes at all

161
00:13:27,600 --> 00:13:30,400
to coming up with the right objective in the first place.

162
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,440
Just thinking doesn’t necessarily buy you the right objective in the first place.

163
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:40,080
I think this is really the key question for any kind of very rapid RSI from current AIs.

164
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:43,040
How well can AIs generalize to learning their own objectives?

165
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:47,200
To have any kind of self-propelling automated loop, we need the AI to propose objectives,

166
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:50,720
optimize them, figure that out, propose a new objective, and have this not go off

167
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:55,120
the rails at any point for a long, long time. To come back to Moravec’s paradox, there might

168
00:13:55,120 --> 00:14:01,200
be a case of Moravec’s paradox where we think this kind of autonomy and being self-encapsulated — so

169
00:14:01,200 --> 00:14:04,960
we can think of what we should do ourselves and then go do it and have this loop — is super

170
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:07,520
easy because we always do this. Obviously, evolution needs to

171
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:10,720
create creatures that can survive by themselves for long periods of time.

172
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:14,000
And this just might be something that for some reason is really hard for the AI, in the same

173
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,040
way that locomotion stuff is really hard but math is super easy despite being super hard for us.

174
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:21,520
But doesn’t the time horizon increasing suggest that that’s—

175
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:26,480
Yeah, exactly. This is another possibility, but I agree, there’s no obvious evidence for this.

176
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:30,800
In fact, the fact that our agents are now super persistent and it’s quite easy to

177
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:35,280
do this is kind of evidence against this. But this would potentially be one of the

178
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:39,280
reasons why we just don’t get this immediate takeoff, if this is hard.

179
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:45,040
If you look back from 2012 till now — or maybe from when you started doing

180
00:14:45,040 --> 00:14:53,600
your research till now — what part of all the innovations that have happened since that time,

181
00:14:53,600 --> 00:14:58,960
including purely engineering ones, including purely conceptual ones, seems like the thing

182
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:05,360
that would be the last thing humans would have to do before AI totally automates AI R&D?

183
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:08,000
Probably just iteratively asking the right questions.

184
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,400
If you can get the AI to do any experiment, you still need to decide what experiments to do.

185
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:16,240
Right now I think AIs are not very good at this compared to coding the experiment.

186
00:15:17,360 --> 00:15:20,240
Whenever we talk about research, they propose a bunch of miscellaneous

187
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:24,640
things which are very, very tiny steps. Or even going from DeepMind’s approach of,

188
00:15:24,640 --> 00:15:30,000
"We’re going to solve intelligence by learning to play games at a superhuman level," to one

189
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
random researcher like Radford being, "I’m going to try and just predict the

190
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,480
next token of a very wide swath of data"… Even once Radford had discovered that,

191
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:43,360
it took a while before people decided to scale it up, because we had to come up

192
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:46,720
with the idea of scaling laws and the fact that you could very reliably predict these things.

193
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:55,520
I would say that the last job for humans, or the role for humans that’ll last the longest,

194
00:15:55,520 --> 00:16:00,560
is defining the objective and deciding what we actually want.

195
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:12,000
In that vein, something like deciding how the AI assistants should behave, or what it means

196
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:17,600
to be helpful, or what the objective is when we’re doing RL from human feedback, is one such thing.

197
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:24,080
Then later, defining constitutions and model specs is another one.

198
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:29,600
Even if the AIs can do all the technical work, we’ll still have to do a lot of that

199
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:33,120
and decide what we actually want. Alignment is the final job.

200
00:16:33,760 --> 00:16:38,960
Alignment is sort of the answer. But alignment itself can be decomposed into

201
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:45,760
specification of the objective, or figuring out what the right objective should be, and then

202
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:51,680
actually achieving or optimizing the objective you’ve defined.

203
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:56,080
I think the first one is not going to go away anytime soon.

204
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:04,640
If I think about a post-training team and why you need a lot of people on the team,

205
00:17:05,360 --> 00:17:09,360
it’s just because there are a lot of different areas where you have

206
00:17:09,360 --> 00:17:19,280
to figure out how the model should behave. It would be very hard to automate the whole thing,

207
00:17:19,280 --> 00:17:24,320
just because someone has to think about how the model should behave in this area.

208
00:17:24,320 --> 00:17:28,640
Jane Street started using Antithesis to test its software in early 2025,

209
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:32,320
and the team was so impressed by the product that it decided to invest in the company.

210
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:36,000
I recently caught up with Ron Minsky, who co-leads Jane Street’s tech group,

211
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:40,560
to ask how Antithesis actually plugs in. The thing that I think is most impressive

212
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:44,640
about Antithesis is that we started using it on a team that was building

213
00:17:44,640 --> 00:17:50,160
high-assurance software and being really careful. Nonetheless, it was able to shake out bugs that

214
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:54,640
were otherwise going to be really hard to find. That’s important both because it helps make

215
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:59,600
those systems more reliable and because it helps the teams that build them move faster.

216
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:03,760
This matters more and more as code production is increasingly automated.I think, in general,

217
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:08,320
as we’ve been using agents more and more, the key problem you run into is the verification

218
00:18:08,320 --> 00:18:12,480
bottleneck: just the time it takes for people to look at code and figure out,

219
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:16,240
is that actually something you want to accept into your production software?

220
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:19,680
Tools that make testing better are just incredibly helpful there.

221
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:23,680
They ease the verification bottleneck and make it possible for you to get more stuff

222
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:27,600
done and move faster, because you can have more confidence that the code generated

223
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:32,960
by the agent isn’t introducing new problems. To see how Antithesis fits into your development

224
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:43,200
process, go to antithesis.com/dwarkesh. What is the story for why there isn’t huge

225
00:18:43,200 --> 00:18:45,920
consolidation in model providers? There are just so many things

226
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:51,920
that point to centralization here. If you step back over the course of years,

227
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:57,360
is there something that is going to prevent that? I think distillation is the main thing that

228
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:02,000
fights against the centralizing force. Basically anything that can be learned

229
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:09,200
through RL can be distilled very easily, because it’s a small number of bits.

230
00:19:09,200 --> 00:19:11,680
It’s something that you can learn from a small amount of data.

231
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:19,440
If you can get trajectories from the model that show a behavior, you can easily distill it.

232
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:26,640
I think distillation is one of the things that fights centralization.

233
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:34,800
There is also a possibility that there’ll be company-specific models, that it’ll be

234
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:41,680
possible to learn from deployment and have a company continually improving its own model.

235
00:19:42,960 --> 00:19:47,520
Such a system could be provided by the current oligopoly of model providers

236
00:19:47,520 --> 00:19:54,240
or some other currently smaller company. But I think that’ll change the game a bit.

237
00:19:54,240 --> 00:19:58,000
I also want to point out that continual learning, honestly, doesn’t stop distillation.

238
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,440
Even if your model is improving every day, people could be distilling it every day.

239
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:09,040
The loops could just operate at the same pace. That makes sense. So copying model behavior…

240
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:14,640
I guess you need to know yourself what the right distribution to prompt is in order to

241
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:19,120
get the relevant model behavior? Oh, yeah. For just distilling

242
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:23,360
with supervised learning, the prompt distribution is extremely important.

243
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:28,240
It’s very non-trivial to distill a model, even if you have full access to it and have

244
00:20:28,240 --> 00:20:35,840
the chain of thought and everything. It’s non-trivial to distill all of the

245
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:40,320
useful capabilities from it, because you need to prompt the model with something.

246
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:47,200
You need to prompt it with realistic prompts. You need to have a really wide

247
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:52,560
distribution of realistic prompts. One thing that’s been coming out recently is

248
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:59,680
that some of the Chinese companies are probably using these router services which are designed

249
00:20:59,680 --> 00:21:05,280
to allow people in China to use the US frontier models, which would otherwise be blocked in China.

250
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:09,440
There are all these router or proxy services that allow people in China

251
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:15,280
to use these models, mostly for coding. And these router services are collecting

252
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:20,400
and selling some of the data. This is a very useful data set

253
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:25,840
for distillation because it gives you the perfect prompt distribution.

254
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:28,080
I think this is one of those things where AIs help a lot.

255
00:21:28,080 --> 00:21:31,760
If you actually look at the frontier pipelines, or the Chinese models that

256
00:21:31,760 --> 00:21:36,880
they’ve actually put in their papers, they get seed prompts from somewhere, which is some

257
00:21:36,880 --> 00:21:40,640
combination of humans and this kind of data. Then they synthesize a vast coverage from

258
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:43,920
those seed prompts using their existing models or the other frontier models.

259
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:48,960
You can automate an awful lot of this prompt distribution gathering and environment creation.

260
00:21:49,520 --> 00:21:52,880
Humans need to provide increasingly fewer bits as the models get better.

261
00:21:53,520 --> 00:22:01,360
But it still seems you’re bottlenecked by having a service which has users going through it.

262
00:22:01,360 --> 00:22:04,320
Not necessarily. That’s obviously very helpful, but theoretically,

263
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:10,640
you can just think about what users want. But the whole point is that the user says,

264
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:13,040
"Make me an application like this. Oh, that didn’t work. I actually want

265
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:15,120
you to make this new feature. But actually,

266
00:22:15,120 --> 00:22:19,040
let’s step back and do this other thing." Capturing that whole trace is the thing.

267
00:22:19,040 --> 00:22:23,440
Or to the extent you could have done that anyway, then you just have RSI.

268
00:22:24,880 --> 00:22:28,480
Ultimately, if you have this fully automated loop, that is basically RSI.

269
00:22:28,480 --> 00:22:30,480
The AI is deciding the data, it’s deciding the training.

270
00:22:30,480 --> 00:22:34,480
That is the loop. But it depends how much human information you need.

271
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:36,640
At some point, if you’re just like, "I want traces that look

272
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:39,520
like this," you prompt that to the model. The model will be able to come up with a

273
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:41,200
pretty good approximation. But what if you want to do,

274
00:22:41,200 --> 00:22:47,280
"Make me a really good politician," and then it has to anticipate de novo how a discussion in the

275
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:51,154
Senate halls would go or something? I just feel like there are going

276
00:22:51,154 --> 00:22:52,480
to be a lot of things which are— Ironically, this is actually easier for

277
00:22:52,480 --> 00:22:55,440
the distillers than the frontier labs. The distiller’s just like,

278
00:22:55,440 --> 00:22:57,680
"I want a good politician." They go to the frontier model.

279
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:01,040
The frontier model already knows how to be a good politician, so it just generates those traces.

280
00:23:01,040 --> 00:23:03,760
Whereas if you actually want to build the first model that does this,

281
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:08,400
you have to actually somehow get data on what politicians do every day and build that.

282
00:23:08,400 --> 00:23:11,510
It’s actually much easier to say, "I want something like this," and then get the AI to

283
00:23:11,510 --> 00:23:15,519
produce a billion variations, than to actually create the thing like this to begin with.

284
00:23:15,519 --> 00:23:20,631
I think you can actually make a really concrete prediction based off this observation

285
00:23:20,631 --> 00:23:25,120
that the Chinese labs have this router data. The thing that started this originally was I

286
00:23:25,120 --> 00:23:32,000
was saying, "Isn’t it weird how Sonnet 5 and Opus 5 are almost objectively worse models

287
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:37,440
than GLM-5.3 and Kimi K3, even though they’ve had access to not only distillation but logit

288
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:40,800
distillation from Mythos?" The counter was that the

289
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:45,360
prompt distribution really, really matters. You need to see what users are doing so that you

290
00:23:45,360 --> 00:23:50,080
can distill these behaviors and things in. I think the prediction from this is that the

291
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:55,280
frontier labs don’t necessarily have much of an advantage, if at all, in RL environments now.

292
00:23:56,000 --> 00:24:01,280
Yes, user distribution matters for general behavior and so on, but the best measure

293
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:05,360
of a capability is the very, very hard RL environments you’ve made at the frontier.

294
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:08,480
If you have access to those RL environments as Anthropic,

295
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:13,200
and you have access to logit distillation, and you’ve still made a worse model, then maybe—

296
00:24:13,200 --> 00:24:15,760
Then real-world deployment matters more than the environment.

297
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:20,560
That’s really interesting. But they had to incentivize those capabilities in the

298
00:24:20,560 --> 00:24:26,480
first place in Fable, or the frontier model. So it’s weird that they can’t incentivize them

299
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:32,800
again with a smaller model or something. Maybe we’re just in this weird uncanny

300
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:38,640
valley where trying to copy that frontier model too much, the student-teacher gap,

301
00:24:38,640 --> 00:24:42,160
whatever it is, is just too large. People have made this point with Opus.

302
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:47,600
The difference between Opus 4.6 and Opus 5 is that Opus 5 really feels like it’s

303
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:50,640
got this AI-as-a-judge checking every possible thing it’s done.

304
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:54,080
That’s why it uses so many tokens. It tries to think about all these things,

305
00:24:54,080 --> 00:24:58,560
but it doesn’t necessarily have the big model smell of Fable to know when to stop doing that,

306
00:24:58,560 --> 00:25:02,000
or when’s a good path to go down. The reach exceeds the grasp.

307
00:25:02,720 --> 00:25:09,120
I would offer a slightly different hypothesis. I would say there are a couple of different axes

308
00:25:09,120 --> 00:25:13,600
for the environments you can create. One of them is difficulty

309
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:19,600
and the other is realism. It’s comparatively easy to create a

310
00:25:19,600 --> 00:25:29,440
lot of difficult environments that involve doing a much more complicated task or doing something that

311
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:33,680
requires a lot more cleverness. You could say this is the

312
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:38,480
benchmaxxing distribution, because a lot of the most prominent benchmarks just involve doing some

313
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:45,280
very hard puzzle-like task that’s easy to verify. Then there’s the realism axis, where you want the

314
00:25:45,280 --> 00:25:49,760
model to be good in the realistic coding agent setting where there’s multiple back-and-forths

315
00:25:49,760 --> 00:26:01,280
with the human and there’s multiple objectives. The labs who are crafting the model behavior for

316
00:26:01,280 --> 00:26:05,600
the first time need to push in both directions. To get good model behavior, you need to really

317
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:11,600
push on the realism axis and have rubrics or some kind of human feedback that’s informing

318
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:18,960
the reward function you use there. But if you try to do distillation naively,

319
00:26:18,960 --> 00:26:23,280
you end up just matching the teacher on the benchmaxxing distribution.

320
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:30,080
If you don’t have enough of the environments that really exercise the capabilities in these

321
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:36,160
trickier realistic settings, then you’re not going to get those into your student model.

322
00:26:36,160 --> 00:26:41,120
I think maybe one thing that’s happening is the big models generalize better from the

323
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:52,480
tricky narrow tasks to these more realistic tasks. If you have a really good realistic prompt

324
00:26:52,480 --> 00:26:56,400
distribution for distillation, you can match the big model really well.

325
00:26:56,400 --> 00:27:03,280
But if you only have this distribution of easily verifiable tasks, then you can match

326
00:27:03,280 --> 00:27:09,680
the big model on all the benchmarks, but you do worse on this broader distribution.

327
00:27:10,960 --> 00:27:17,760
That might even explain something about the smaller Anthropic models, like Sonnet 5,

328
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:22,080
though it’s hard to predict exactly what they’re doing to post-train those models.

329
00:27:22,080 --> 00:27:28,480
It could also be that they’re always changing their post-training stack, and they just got

330
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:37,200
a few things wrong in some of these models. I don’t know… they turned something up too

331
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:39,920
high and created some quirks that people really don’t like.

332
00:27:39,920 --> 00:27:44,640
It’s really easy to screw up post-training in some way that doesn’t show up in benchmarks.

333
00:27:45,280 --> 00:27:50,000
Just one other very basic point is that the frontier AI labs buy

334
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,960
all their data from big data companies. The Chinese can also just buy the same

335
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:54,560
data from data companies. And they are, right?

336
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:57,520
And they are. Exactly. There’s a lot of people being annoyed about this,

337
00:27:57,520 --> 00:28:02,080
but if they have exactly the same data and they can buy that, they can also distill.

338
00:28:02,080 --> 00:28:11,200
It means it’s quite easy to keep up, really. The other question I had is how the first

339
00:28:11,200 --> 00:28:15,360
models that are capable of automating AI R&D will actually be trained.

340
00:28:15,360 --> 00:28:19,760
There’s a toy version, which is this thing that Ryan was talking about.

341
00:28:19,760 --> 00:28:26,160
You just have GPT-8 try to build GPT-3 size models that are really good at inner loop

342
00:28:26,160 --> 00:28:32,400
type challenges: beating video games that require continual learning, or just getting to a certain

343
00:28:32,400 --> 00:28:36,640
loss with the least amount of compute, et cetera. But John, I think you had an interesting point

344
00:28:36,640 --> 00:28:39,760
that maybe that’s not the way it actually will happen in practice.

345
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:44,240
So I’m curious, by the point at which you have AIs that are actually capable of automating AI R&D,

346
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:47,680
how are they probably trained? We’ll probably do some combination

347
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:52,720
of learning from human feedback to absorb the researchers’ taste,

348
00:28:52,720 --> 00:28:59,600
and just creating a lot of practice environments which involve doing multi-step research projects.

349
00:29:03,360 --> 00:29:06,560
People will in practice do some combination of those two things and,

350
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:13,520
each iteration, patch whatever seems to be most broken in the last iteration.

351
00:29:14,960 --> 00:29:22,240
Researchers will be using the AIs a lot and will notice that they have some consistent weaknesses.

352
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:28,720
Those things will either be patched by collecting human feedback or creating environments.

353
00:29:29,840 --> 00:29:33,120
Maybe a useful way to think about this is how much of the lineage we

354
00:29:33,120 --> 00:29:38,880
roll back and then let self-play from there. In the limit, you’re picturing just giving

355
00:29:38,880 --> 00:29:44,800
them a GPU and maybe neural nets or something and saying, "Okay, figure out how to train

356
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:49,600
a model to do these particular tasks." The way it currently works is we go up

357
00:29:49,600 --> 00:29:54,320
to the very edge of the lineage and say, "Okay, here are the bugs Anthropic has found in their

358
00:29:54,320 --> 00:29:56,400
training stack in the last few months. We’ll turn those into environments."

359
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:59,440
You need to train and get better on the frontier.

360
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:01,840
So you obviously lock in all the previous history of the lineage.

361
00:30:01,840 --> 00:30:06,720
But you could imagine a world in which you roll back to before GRPO or something.

362
00:30:06,720 --> 00:30:12,640
Then you have environments which try to get it to discover the best form to RL models on, and

363
00:30:12,640 --> 00:30:16,800
then maybe you roll further and further back… But I think we will still be so compute bottlenecked

364
00:30:16,800 --> 00:30:21,360
that people will just keep staying at the frontier and essentially diffing the bugs and whatever

365
00:30:21,360 --> 00:30:24,560
improvements they found since the last model version, turning those into training environments.

366
00:30:24,560 --> 00:30:29,520
Which is also really good for having non-stale, new data between model generations.

367
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:35,680
Again, this is basically continual learning within the AI lab, of distilling the last three months of

368
00:30:35,680 --> 00:30:41,680
AI research progress through environments and RLHF-type stuff back into the model itself.

369
00:30:41,680 --> 00:30:46,480
And it is distilling. That’s maybe why some of us feel like it’s asymptotic.

370
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:50,240
You’re always just trying to get the last three months of progress.

371
00:30:50,240 --> 00:30:54,480
That progress is being contributed to by AIs, of course, but it also still has humans in the loop.

372
00:30:54,480 --> 00:30:58,480
It feels like you’re just constantly inching closer and closer to what the human researchers

373
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:01,680
are finding and capable of doing. The one thing I will say, though,

374
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:04,800
is obviously if you’re just distilling on trajectories, you can never go above it.

375
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:07,840
But environments can go quite a far way above what a human can do.

376
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:10,720
It’s very easy to design an environment that no human can solve, but the AI can

377
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:14,080
obviously still try and solve it. That would be the path to go ahead

378
00:31:14,080 --> 00:31:18,400
of just what the human AI research is. Do you have an example in terms of RSI,

379
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:21,200
of what kind of training set? Nanochat speedrun, but doing it

380
00:31:21,200 --> 00:31:24,880
even faster than a human speedrunner. I feel like in AI research especially,

381
00:31:24,880 --> 00:31:29,360
it’s very easy to define goals. You could say the loss needs to be 1.3 or

382
00:31:29,360 --> 00:31:34,160
something, and no human can get that now. But that’s an extremely measurable,

383
00:31:34,160 --> 00:31:37,120
verifiable task. If the AI gets that, then great.

384
00:31:37,120 --> 00:31:41,360
Or I don’t know, building a 100 million parameter model that beats Minecraft.

385
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:45,680
That’s maybe too easy, but beats a much more complicated game or something.

386
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:48,880
Isn’t it crazy that 100 million parameter models beat Minecraft?

387
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:52,400
We’re calling that too easy? Imagine if you said that five years ago.

388
00:31:52,400 --> 00:31:56,000
I would say a lot of research is not exactly like that, though,

389
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:01,760
where it’s hill climbing on a well-defined goal. It’s more like, here’s an intuition we have about

390
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:07,200
some way models should be better. We also have some idea for an algorithm

391
00:32:07,200 --> 00:32:15,040
that seems to go a little bit in this direction. So let’s come up with a task that is designed

392
00:32:15,040 --> 00:32:22,160
to show signs of life on this approach, and see if we get those signs of life.

393
00:32:22,160 --> 00:32:26,080
If we do, we can make successively more realistic versions of the task.

394
00:32:26,080 --> 00:32:35,440
It’s a lot more guided by intuition. The inner loop is to test for that

395
00:32:35,440 --> 00:32:40,240
intuition rather than the test itself leading to the insight.

396
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:45,120
Right. You’re not directly optimizing for the eventual objective you care

397
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:54,000
about or the practical production objective. You’re relaxing your objective a little bit.

398
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:59,440
You’re saying, "Let’s relax on the realism axis a little bit and find some methods that actually

399
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:04,400
work, and then try to get back to realism later after the method matures a little bit."

400
00:33:05,200 --> 00:33:13,760
There’s also research that’s more oriented towards explaining things and developing a theory.

401
00:33:15,680 --> 00:33:20,800
Often we don’t have mathematical theories in machine learning that are that predictive.

402
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:26,480
But we have a lot of more informal theories for what’s going on.

403
00:33:27,120 --> 00:33:30,480
Presumably the models will be trained on some combination of all of these tasks.

404
00:33:30,480 --> 00:33:34,960
Some will be very easily verifiable, some will be LLM-as-a-judge or just ask the human,

405
00:33:34,960 --> 00:33:39,520
"Does this look reasonable?" The hope would be that these would

406
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:43,200
all generalize to these much harder, more vague, fuzzy kinds of tasks.

407
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:45,680
It probably will to some extent. Whether it generalizes enough that

408
00:33:46,640 --> 00:33:50,560
the loop can become self-sealing without humans being in the loop at all is unclear.

409
00:33:51,760 --> 00:33:55,360
Maybe taking a step back. Here’s what it seems to me

410
00:33:55,360 --> 00:34:00,800
the plan for AI research going forward is. You tell me if you think it’s going to work

411
00:34:00,800 --> 00:34:08,080
or if you agree with this characterization. The bet is that we will scale up RLVR training

412
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:13,360
across millions of diverse environments, across hundreds of different kinds of domains.

413
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:19,440
What will emerge at the other end is an agent which has learned these basic skills — or less

414
00:34:19,440 --> 00:34:26,240
than basic skills — around being persistent, being able to triage information and context,

415
00:34:26,240 --> 00:34:30,640
eventually having end-to-end optimization of working with other agents and things like that.

416
00:34:31,200 --> 00:34:36,560
Such an agent will be very sample efficient within the context —you've done research on how you

417
00:34:36,560 --> 00:34:40,640
scale up in-context learning to make it arbitrarily long, but you keep scaling it up.

418
00:34:40,640 --> 00:34:45,840
And what comes out the other end will be something that basically functions like a drop-in remote

419
00:34:45,840 --> 00:34:49,760
worker over the course of a week or a month. First of all, do you agree that that is

420
00:34:49,760 --> 00:34:53,520
the bet the labs are making? And second, is that enough?

421
00:34:53,520 --> 00:34:58,240
Basically learning how to learn within these simulacra within a data center,

422
00:34:58,240 --> 00:35:02,400
and then getting deployed into the real world, but not actually learning from real-world deployment…

423
00:35:02,400 --> 00:35:07,520
only learning these meta skills from the simulated environments in the data center.

424
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:13,120
I think it’s now hard to separate out how much of the labs’ effort is going

425
00:35:13,120 --> 00:35:17,520
towards direct RSI versus making generally intelligent models that they can continue

426
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:20,560
to deploy to collect revenue to fund the next big training run.

427
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:30,640
For the latter, yes, that’s probably just the bet they’re making.

428
00:35:30,640 --> 00:35:32,400
It’s very clear, the pattern of where these environments are going over the last few years.

429
00:35:32,400 --> 00:35:34,560
Anthropic’s lineage of environments is a very clear example of this.

430
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:38,880
First, we just focus on coding and we’re going to get really, really good at that.

431
00:35:38,880 --> 00:35:42,560
Then, from the task horizon that we’ve got from coding — which is probably the

432
00:35:42,560 --> 00:35:46,160
lowest-hanging fruit in terms of data available on the internet to create environments, and

433
00:35:46,160 --> 00:35:50,080
their own internal stuff that they can turn into environments — then we’re going to generalize.

434
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:54,400
We’re going to go up to finance next, and literally just so much Excel data and all

435
00:35:54,400 --> 00:35:58,640
that sort of stuff in the RL training. Then it’s PowerPoints. It’s this long

436
00:35:58,640 --> 00:36:02,160
tail of the working economy. That seemed to work really well.

437
00:36:02,160 --> 00:36:05,200
A lot of the other labs, even the open source labs, have now realized

438
00:36:05,200 --> 00:36:07,920
that that was the correct bet to make. But what is the implication from that?

439
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:12,160
When I had Dario on the podcast, the thing I asked him was, if you truly expect models which

440
00:36:12,160 --> 00:36:18,560
will be human-like in their ability to learn on the job, why would you try to bake in all these

441
00:36:18,560 --> 00:36:22,080
skills of working with PowerPoint or something? Wouldn’t you just expect the model to be able

442
00:36:22,080 --> 00:36:26,240
to pick that up while it’s deployed? There’s multiple different explanations.

443
00:36:26,240 --> 00:36:31,120
One is just that we expect models to get there soon, but they’re not there yet, so why not

444
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:38,160
amortize these skills into the model training? Another is that we’re not concentrated on making

445
00:36:38,160 --> 00:36:42,960
it really good at widely deployed work. We just want it really good at RSI.

446
00:36:43,760 --> 00:36:48,400
This is just a way for us to get revenue so that we can pour it back into a model that is actually

447
00:36:48,400 --> 00:36:52,640
really good at doing RSI development. Then once the singularity happens,

448
00:36:52,640 --> 00:36:56,080
the thing that comes out the other end will be really good at all the things which seem like

449
00:36:56,080 --> 00:36:59,520
bottlenecks to the current generation of models. John, I don’t know if you have takes on

450
00:37:00,720 --> 00:37:06,160
how one should construe why there is so much task-specific knowledge in these models if the

451
00:37:06,160 --> 00:37:10,480
path is this kind of generalization. If the models were good enough at

452
00:37:10,480 --> 00:37:15,440
learning in context, then in theory, you wouldn’t need to train them on finance.

453
00:37:15,440 --> 00:37:22,880
They would just be able to read all the books on the fly and figure out how to do everything

454
00:37:22,880 --> 00:37:29,360
in the appropriate jurisdiction. You could argue that you need to do

455
00:37:29,360 --> 00:37:34,240
a lot of this domain-specific training just to make them more efficient.

456
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:40,240
Even if they were smart enough to figure this out on the fly, you still might want to do a bunch of

457
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:47,760
RL and bake all these intuitions into the weights, so the model would be more efficient at runtime.

458
00:37:49,040 --> 00:37:55,040
In practice, it does seem like model providers are going domain by domain and trying to strengthen

459
00:37:55,040 --> 00:37:59,680
the models in the highest value domains. I’d say that that’s one of the answers to

460
00:37:59,680 --> 00:38:04,880
why the models have gotten so much better. It’s just because the model providers have

461
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:10,720
covered a lot of the high-value domains and the most common types of skills.

462
00:38:10,720 --> 00:38:14,080
Another thing is just that it’s not that expensive to do both at the same time.

463
00:38:14,640 --> 00:38:18,560
The models are massive. They can easily afford, in terms of their parameters, to learn everything.

464
00:38:18,560 --> 00:38:22,480
There is likely some transfer. Even if finance is not specific,

465
00:38:22,480 --> 00:38:26,640
the information is important for RSI. Just the general meta-learning of how to figure

466
00:38:26,640 --> 00:38:31,440
out what’s important, how to have taste, how to do long-horizon work is potentially generalizable.

467
00:38:31,440 --> 00:38:34,560
There’s not that much RSI data in the world as well.

468
00:38:34,560 --> 00:38:36,960
It’s hard to generate and requires a lot of effort.

469
00:38:36,960 --> 00:38:40,480
So if you can amortize in this other data, you get some transfer from it.

470
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:42,800
You already have masses of compute and masses of parameter space,

471
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:47,200
so why not do that as well as, obviously, the direct commercial intent of selling a model?

472
00:38:48,320 --> 00:38:55,760
I’ll add that there’s one question about whether this current paradigm of doing

473
00:38:55,760 --> 00:39:03,040
sim-to-real will be the dominant one forever. You look at what the real-world tasks are like.

474
00:39:03,040 --> 00:39:06,320
Then you try to create a bunch of environments that can be simulated

475
00:39:07,040 --> 00:39:14,160
in the data center, and you can do RL on them. Obviously, this has been very successful.

476
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:19,760
But it has a lot of weaknesses, because a lot of things are just hard to simulate,

477
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:25,440
especially if they involve interacting with a bunch of humans in real time.

478
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:31,200
So there’s some question about whether sim-to-real will be the dominant framework forever.

479
00:39:31,200 --> 00:39:34,800
I think sim-to-real has to be the dominant framework while sample efficiency is low,

480
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:38,560
because right now you need thousands and thousands of interactions with the humans.

481
00:39:38,560 --> 00:39:42,720
No human is going to sit there and be in the loop of RL training.

482
00:39:42,720 --> 00:39:45,760
So we have to simulate that now to get the samples you need.

483
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:48,880
But obviously, if sample efficiency improves a lot, you’d expect learning from

484
00:39:48,880 --> 00:39:53,760
deployment to become a much bigger part of it. Though there are also other things you could do.

485
00:39:53,760 --> 00:39:59,360
You can learn off-policy, so you can take all the traces, and even without resimulating everything,

486
00:39:59,360 --> 00:40:03,760
you can potentially learn something from them. Jane Street just launched a new competition,

487
00:40:03,760 --> 00:40:08,240
and it’s its most ambitious one yet: design a protocol-emulator ASIC.

488
00:40:08,240 --> 00:40:11,680
Basically, if you have a chip you want to test, you can connect it to this ASIC,

489
00:40:11,680 --> 00:40:16,720
and the ASIC will simulate realistic traffic. That way, you can see how the chip responds

490
00:40:16,720 --> 00:40:20,560
without having to plug it into a live system. Jane Street is looking for flexible,

491
00:40:20,560 --> 00:40:23,920
general-purpose designs, not single-protocol emulators.

492
00:40:23,920 --> 00:40:28,160
When I was chatting with them, they suggested that I start by trying to implement what are apparently

493
00:40:28,160 --> 00:40:35,040
three very common protocols: UART, SPI, and I²C. Jane Street also mentioned that they hope more

494
00:40:35,040 --> 00:40:41,040
ambitious designs will tackle low-speed USB, Ethernet, and any other protocols that flex

495
00:40:41,040 --> 00:40:44,880
your chip’s specific architecture. Importantly, your design should be

496
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:49,280
reprogrammable rather than smashing a bunch of specific protocols onto a chip.

497
00:40:49,280 --> 00:40:53,920
If a new protocol comes out after your ASIC is taped out, your chip

498
00:40:53,920 --> 00:40:57,360
still needs to be able to handle it. How exactly it does that is up to you.

499
00:40:57,360 --> 00:41:03,920
But there is one hard constraint: your design must target an open-source 130-nanometer process node.

500
00:41:03,920 --> 00:41:07,280
That’s because Jane Street will pay to tape out the most novel submissions

501
00:41:07,280 --> 00:41:12,320
and send physical copies to the winners. The competition is open until January 18,

502
00:41:12,320 --> 00:41:19,920
2027, and working in teams is highly encouraged. Go to janestreet.com/dwarkesh to download the

503
00:41:19,920 --> 00:41:23,600
template code and get started. I want to ask more about this,

504
00:41:23,600 --> 00:41:30,000
because it’s weird that you have 50% of compute that’s spent on inference that is

505
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:35,440
not directly helping the model become better. One of the key advantages you’d expect digital

506
00:41:35,440 --> 00:41:38,880
minds to eventually have is that, unlike a human who gets to have 50

507
00:41:38,880 --> 00:41:43,600
years of real-world experience, a model will get to experience, through all its instances,

508
00:41:45,520 --> 00:41:51,920
millions of years of deployment across all kinds of economically relevant work in the economy.

509
00:41:51,920 --> 00:41:56,080
Right now, that data is just not, in a meaningful sense, helping the model get better.

510
00:41:56,080 --> 00:42:00,320
It seems so obvious that eventually models should be able to learn from this data.

511
00:42:00,320 --> 00:42:03,840
Once they do, you would have something that almost feels like a widely deployed

512
00:42:03,840 --> 00:42:07,920
intelligence explosion, because the model is assimilating so much information

513
00:42:07,920 --> 00:42:11,600
across all these deployed instances. When do you expect this kind of hive-mind,

514
00:42:11,600 --> 00:42:15,440
crazy shit to start happening? I think broadly, at a very basic level,

515
00:42:15,440 --> 00:42:18,640
this is already happening… just in the next generation of models.

516
00:42:18,640 --> 00:42:21,920
Right now, you can obviously take your deployment data and put this in the pre-train or the

517
00:42:21,920 --> 00:42:26,000
mid-train of future models, especially if you do some kind of filtering or some kind of judgment

518
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:30,160
or annotation or synthesization of that. How much do you think that explains the

519
00:42:30,160 --> 00:42:33,200
generation-over-generation improvement? I think it explains quite a bit.

520
00:42:35,360 --> 00:42:37,920
I don’t know whether the labs do this, because theoretically, they claim not

521
00:42:37,920 --> 00:42:41,200
to train on people’s data. But the Chinese 100% do.

522
00:42:41,200 --> 00:42:45,200
They definitely get this advantage. This is basically what distillation is.

523
00:42:45,200 --> 00:42:50,000
They take the models, they get some fraction of their deployment data by pinging the model,

524
00:42:50,000 --> 00:42:52,240
and then they train their next generation of models on it.

525
00:42:52,240 --> 00:42:54,080
They can certainly do it on their own models as well.

526
00:42:54,080 --> 00:42:56,560
There’s no reason not to whatsoever. I completely agree with this.

527
00:42:56,560 --> 00:42:59,280
If you zoom out far enough, this is definitely happening.

528
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:06,400
What we’re all picturing, the holy grail of continual learning, is this very organic,

529
00:43:06,400 --> 00:43:10,480
live loop of an individual model getting an experience and live-updating on the

530
00:43:10,480 --> 00:43:13,200
spot and learning from that. A lot of things break when you

531
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:16,560
zoom into that level of granularity. But the big labs are doing this.

532
00:43:17,200 --> 00:43:19,520
The closed models are doing this. There are also early signs of life

533
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:23,200
of people using open-source models doing this at a much faster cadence.

534
00:43:24,240 --> 00:43:27,520
A good example is probably Composer. Harvey’s doing the same thing with legal agents.

535
00:43:27,520 --> 00:43:38,000
You have some sort of model, and you are getting very specific environments from the

536
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:42,400
data that you have for that particular task, and things that users are complaining about,

537
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:46,880
and all the feedback that you’re somehow extracting from your specific deployments.

538
00:43:46,880 --> 00:43:49,520
A lot of these companies have the advantage over the big labs in that they

539
00:43:49,520 --> 00:43:52,720
can use this data really, really well. Then they will create environments.

540
00:43:52,720 --> 00:43:57,040
They will do a big post-train of Kimi K3. They will go deploy it.

541
00:43:57,040 --> 00:44:00,320
They might do some online learning as well, like Composer did online…

542
00:44:00,320 --> 00:44:04,880
basically REINFORCE for a long time. There’s still a human in the loop.

543
00:44:04,880 --> 00:44:07,120
There’s still a human saying, "Okay, these are the signals we care about.

544
00:44:07,120 --> 00:44:09,680
Here’s how we’re going to create environments from the data that we have."

545
00:44:09,680 --> 00:44:12,640
It’s still a longer cadence than maybe the one that you’re thinking of,

546
00:44:12,640 --> 00:44:15,280
but it really is happening. Eventually that loop will

547
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:17,680
become faster and faster. The Composer thing is interesting

548
00:44:17,680 --> 00:44:24,240
because this is where, in Cursor, people press Tab or they don’t press Tab on the

549
00:44:24,240 --> 00:44:28,240
next completion that the model suggests. Based on that, every single day, Composer

550
00:44:28,240 --> 00:44:31,520
gets better at predicting the next— That was the old Tab model.

551
00:44:31,520 --> 00:44:35,920
They actually did the same thing not just for the Tab model, but for the actual generative model.

552
00:44:35,920 --> 00:44:39,120
Oh, I see. Interesting. It’s hard because when you do online

553
00:44:39,120 --> 00:44:42,560
reinforcement learning, you don’t have groups. You just have one user saying one thing,

554
00:44:42,560 --> 00:44:47,040
and then you get one rollout. So you have a big variance-reduction problem.

555
00:44:47,040 --> 00:44:53,600
Cursor’s fuzzy answer to this was, "We have very good heuristics which are able to estimate how

556
00:44:53,600 --> 00:44:57,440
much better than average this response was, or how much worse than average this response was."

557
00:44:57,440 --> 00:45:03,360
Then they would do this big REINFORCE update. Their solution to whether it got worse or not

558
00:45:03,360 --> 00:45:07,040
was that if it improved on CursorBench, they would deploy the new model every five hours.

559
00:45:07,040 --> 00:45:11,040
If it didn’t, they would throw that version out. I think your biggest problem is actually

560
00:45:11,040 --> 00:45:14,000
just not knowing what the reward function should be for natural data.

561
00:45:14,640 --> 00:45:21,520
If you use some kind of superficial signal, like did they accept the edit,

562
00:45:22,560 --> 00:45:27,520
that might get reward-hacked in some way. But isn’t this a bigger issue with the sim-to-real

563
00:45:27,520 --> 00:45:34,160
thing, where the longer-horizon tasks get, the harder they are to simulate within a data center?

564
00:45:34,800 --> 00:45:42,080
It seems to me that even in coding, we’re already getting to the point where there’s not some

565
00:45:42,080 --> 00:45:47,360
year-long coding task that doesn’t eventually require you to talk to a client or interact

566
00:45:47,360 --> 00:45:51,840
with the company or interact with users. If you think about the gamut of things we

567
00:45:51,840 --> 00:45:56,480
would want AI to be capable of, eventually superintelligence should be able to run a

568
00:45:56,480 --> 00:46:00,160
business, or start a new business and make it profitable, or have a profitable day trading

569
00:46:00,160 --> 00:46:03,600
in the markets, or win a court case. These are all things which are very

570
00:46:03,600 --> 00:46:07,120
hard to simulate in a data center. An inherent part of the learning

571
00:46:07,120 --> 00:46:11,840
there is interacting with the real world. Maybe they learn how to get better at these

572
00:46:11,840 --> 00:46:16,480
things from the transfer between sim-to-real. But alternatively, maybe you do need weight

573
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:21,600
updates from these kinds of interactions in order to get better at them.

574
00:46:21,600 --> 00:46:24,720
If that is the case — if transfer isn’t strong enough and you do need weight

575
00:46:24,720 --> 00:46:30,000
updates — then the fact that the models are quite sample-inefficient is maybe a deeper problem.

576
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:34,960
The reason I’m curious about this is that by default, I don’t see how

577
00:46:34,960 --> 00:46:39,520
you don’t get some kind of crazy recursive self-improvement within the next 10 years.

578
00:46:39,520 --> 00:46:45,200
But the one reason why that might not happen is that in terms of the sample efficiency of weight

579
00:46:45,200 --> 00:46:50,080
updates, models just seem way far behind humans. They’re plausibly a millionfold behind humans in

580
00:46:50,080 --> 00:46:53,760
terms of how much data a human sees from birth to adulthood versus how much a model

581
00:46:53,760 --> 00:47:02,480
sees from cold start to finishing training. This is all to say, first of all, is there going

582
00:47:02,480 --> 00:47:05,920
to be good transfer between simulations and the extremely long-horizon, really complicated real

583
00:47:05,920 --> 00:47:10,320
shit that we want the AI to do in the real world? And if not, does that really mean that the

584
00:47:10,320 --> 00:47:13,920
lack of sample efficiency in these models comes to bite us?

585
00:47:14,800 --> 00:47:18,240
Maybe the way I’d break down the two types of tasks — the ones in which models get good

586
00:47:18,240 --> 00:47:22,240
and the ones where models will still continue to struggle — is whether the task is cumulative,

587
00:47:22,240 --> 00:47:26,160
or whether you have this non-stationary distribution where you have to keep

588
00:47:26,160 --> 00:47:31,760
learning and relitigating a bunch of stuff. An example of a cumulative task might be RSI.

589
00:47:32,320 --> 00:47:36,800
It’s theoretically possible to have a less-than-a-million-token Python file

590
00:47:36,800 --> 00:47:41,120
which from scratch trains a model that is capable of recursive self-improvement.

591
00:47:41,680 --> 00:47:45,040
Every discovery that you make is a line in the sand that you hold.

592
00:47:45,040 --> 00:47:49,120
If it’s true that for RSI we don’t need to discover a new attention variant or whatever,

593
00:47:49,120 --> 00:47:52,480
then once you’ve discovered attention, and once you’ve discovered mixture of experts,

594
00:47:52,480 --> 00:47:56,960
and once you discover GRPO, you just add that to the training stack and that’s there.

595
00:47:56,960 --> 00:48:03,520
A good example of this is 5.6 Sol training, 5.6 Terra, or whichever one OpenAI told us it trained.

596
00:48:03,520 --> 00:48:07,680
It didn’t have to go back and discover attention. It basically would have called a bunch of

597
00:48:07,680 --> 00:48:11,600
scripts, like pre-training.sh and post-training.sh, and just done that.

598
00:48:12,160 --> 00:48:16,080
That’s an example of a cumulative task. I think the real world — and the reason

599
00:48:16,080 --> 00:48:20,560
people are thinking so much about continual learning — is not really a cumulative task.

600
00:48:20,560 --> 00:48:25,120
Imagine in a law firm, you have an agent acting as a legal associate.

601
00:48:25,120 --> 00:48:27,200
That’s a very non-stationary distribution.

602
00:48:27,200 --> 00:48:30,400
You have to be able to fit in your context all the relationships between

603
00:48:30,400 --> 00:48:33,680
all the important people at that company, which are also changing all the time.

604
00:48:33,680 --> 00:48:38,960
You have all these implicit ways about how things are done, where to find information, et cetera.

605
00:48:38,960 --> 00:48:43,280
That’s not as clean an example of a cumulative task as RSI is.

606
00:48:43,920 --> 00:48:45,920
I think there will be this breakdown between tasks.

607
00:48:45,920 --> 00:48:50,080
But if the labs realize that — and they do believe that RSI is cumulative in the sense

608
00:48:50,080 --> 00:48:54,480
that we don’t need to go back and discover some brand-new architecture or whatever — then maybe

609
00:48:54,480 --> 00:48:56,960
more and more effort and compute gets focused on that versus the other tasks.

610
00:48:56,960 --> 00:49:01,760
It’s so unfortunate that RSI happened to be easier than being a paralegal.

611
00:49:04,800 --> 00:49:12,080
I would say today’s models are weaker than humans in a lot of different ways.

612
00:49:13,040 --> 00:49:16,960
Some of them might have to do with sample efficiency in a certain regime.

613
00:49:19,200 --> 00:49:23,040
In some regimes, models are very sample-efficient, like learning in context.

614
00:49:23,040 --> 00:49:27,360
But then there might be some medium-length regime where they’re less sample-efficient,

615
00:49:27,360 --> 00:49:34,400
because humans can do some kind of weight update more efficiently than models.

616
00:49:34,960 --> 00:49:41,120
I think being less sample-efficient in certain regimes might be one of the sources of weakness.

617
00:49:41,120 --> 00:49:45,120
But I think there are other sources of weakness that are completely different from that.

618
00:49:45,120 --> 00:49:52,400
For example, having lower diversity of thought than humans, or being bad at

619
00:49:52,400 --> 00:49:58,080
certain kinds of long-horizon judgments. I think a lot of what people call taste

620
00:49:58,080 --> 00:50:05,360
is something about behavior that works in the long run, and that

621
00:50:05,360 --> 00:50:12,720
people have realized works in the long run. Not everything, but some aspect of taste.

622
00:50:12,720 --> 00:50:16,400
Especially for something like software engineering, I think a lot of taste is "What

623
00:50:16,400 --> 00:50:23,520
are the systems that are going to be maintainable and work well in the long run of this project?"

624
00:50:25,200 --> 00:50:31,440
There are a variety of weaknesses of models which limit RSI along with other things.

625
00:50:34,880 --> 00:50:37,520
Some of them are related to sample efficiency, and some of them aren’t.

626
00:50:37,520 --> 00:50:42,720
Maybe an interesting thought experiment is this. Let’s say you were able to give a model a context

627
00:50:42,720 --> 00:50:46,720
window of a trillion tokens, or whatever you would have needed to fit in your

628
00:50:46,720 --> 00:50:51,920
experience prior to, let’s say, RLHF. It’s got all that experience in the

629
00:50:51,920 --> 00:50:55,760
context window, and it has the same sample efficiency and in-context learning ability

630
00:50:55,760 --> 00:50:59,840
as it does at a million tokens. Do you think taste is then solved?

631
00:50:59,840 --> 00:51:02,000
Would it be able to make the same judgments that you did?

632
00:51:02,000 --> 00:51:05,760
Or is there something fundamentally missing, apart from just a longer context window with

633
00:51:05,760 --> 00:51:08,720
the same sample efficiency? It would have to be trained

634
00:51:08,720 --> 00:51:15,840
to learn from that context. Either it would have to be

635
00:51:16,480 --> 00:51:22,139
trained to learn the right update to make from that context, or it would have to generalize.

636
00:51:22,139 --> 00:51:26,240
So you don’t think you can just dump it all in, your whole life, your research experience?

637
00:51:26,240 --> 00:51:28,560
You still need the data to train it on long context.

638
00:51:28,560 --> 00:51:31,760
Even if you could theoretically get a trillion context, you would need a trillion lengths

639
00:51:31,760 --> 00:51:34,560
of data to train it. Right now you have 10k context,

640
00:51:34,560 --> 00:51:37,200
you can't just dump in a million. Yeah, I’m just asking if you had that.

641
00:51:37,200 --> 00:51:39,840
In theory, I think, yes. This really just comes down to the

642
00:51:39,840 --> 00:51:44,640
question of how meta-learnable taste is from shorter-horizon episodes.

643
00:51:44,640 --> 00:51:48,160
I feel like there’s no obvious reason it’s super long, because humans somehow developed taste

644
00:51:48,160 --> 00:51:51,600
without having many long episodes. We don’t live to be 10,000.

645
00:51:53,120 --> 00:51:57,600
We develop pretty quickly. If you think about even a PhD, the difference between

646
00:51:57,600 --> 00:52:02,400
a first-year PhD student and a final-year student or postdoc, that’s five years maybe.

647
00:52:02,400 --> 00:52:05,600
They’ve only done maybe 10-30 research projects in total.

648
00:52:05,600 --> 00:52:10,960
But somehow they develop taste quite quickly from a relatively short succession of small things.

649
00:52:10,960 --> 00:52:13,840
Theoretically, it’s possible to develop it like that.

650
00:52:13,840 --> 00:52:18,080
The AI obviously will have vastly more experience in which to develop taste, to meta-learn it.

651
00:52:18,080 --> 00:52:22,720
Then the question is how well that generalizes to really long-horizon things, which I think is

652
00:52:22,720 --> 00:52:25,360
really unsolved at this point. We don’t know.

653
00:52:25,360 --> 00:52:29,680
Going back to this question, eventually there should be a regime where AIs are

654
00:52:29,680 --> 00:52:33,760
learning a ton from each individual instance of deployment that they have.

655
00:52:33,760 --> 00:52:36,960
Currently you could say there’s a fuzzy meta process

656
00:52:36,960 --> 00:52:43,520
by which models do improve from deployment. But I feel like it’s a very weak feedback loop.

657
00:52:43,520 --> 00:52:48,320
Do you see this on the horizon, where there’s this hive mind kind of learning that’s very rapid,

658
00:52:48,320 --> 00:52:55,680
and if so, how exactly does it happen? I would say that whether we get a hive

659
00:52:55,680 --> 00:53:02,320
mind that learns from all of its deployment experience is in a big part about incentives,

660
00:53:02,320 --> 00:53:07,600
rather than being a technical question. Companies aren’t going to want to

661
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:13,920
have the model provider learn from all of their deployment, because that might just

662
00:53:13,920 --> 00:53:20,480
reduce the advantage of their business. I think the economics of this will pressure,

663
00:53:20,480 --> 00:53:26,720
not necessarily weight updates to one big common shared model, but modules that get subbed in.

664
00:53:26,720 --> 00:53:29,440
A very obvious example of this is a LoRA, but it might be something else.

665
00:53:30,160 --> 00:53:34,640
There’s been a lot of work to try and fit an arbitrary context length into a fixed size.

666
00:53:34,640 --> 00:53:39,280
This is all the linear attention stuff. And cartridges, which are essentially

667
00:53:39,280 --> 00:53:43,680
KV caches trained to be very, very compressed to fit in a lot of information.

668
00:53:43,680 --> 00:53:48,480
That’s another example of something that companies may be willing to sign up for, if that gets

669
00:53:48,480 --> 00:53:51,840
subbed into the model and it’s not actually changing the base underlying model itself.

670
00:53:53,120 --> 00:53:56,960
There are many different versions of learning from your data in real time.

671
00:53:56,960 --> 00:54:00,560
The latter ones are not really helping the big labs, because they are just these modules.

672
00:54:00,560 --> 00:54:06,080
But I think the economic pressure will force the labs to go down that

673
00:54:06,080 --> 00:54:11,360
path first before they can embark on this... Which economic pressure, though? I feel like

674
00:54:11,360 --> 00:54:14,960
even if you have a bunch of cartridges or LoRAs or whatnot, you can still just

675
00:54:14,960 --> 00:54:19,200
take all these traces and dump them into the pre-training of your next generation of models.

676
00:54:19,200 --> 00:54:23,120
Yes. It may be a more indirect form of learning that the big labs are getting.

677
00:54:23,120 --> 00:54:26,640
That’s obviously still really valuable to them. But I can’t imagine a world in which we start

678
00:54:26,640 --> 00:54:29,760
off with, "We’re going to just directly train this one big

679
00:54:29,760 --> 00:54:33,280
model on all the exact data that we’re getting." No, I think it will definitely go through stages,

680
00:54:33,280 --> 00:54:36,720
because this is assuming there’s one discontinuous event where suddenly

681
00:54:36,720 --> 00:54:39,760
we fix weight updates continuously. In practice, I think it’s much more

682
00:54:39,760 --> 00:54:43,200
likely to be that the cartridges and stuff allow you to specialize in deployments.

683
00:54:43,200 --> 00:54:46,160
Then you generate traces, you put that in your model, and three months later you come

684
00:54:46,160 --> 00:54:50,400
out with a model which is better at this stuff. You specialize it again, you consolidate it again,

685
00:54:50,400 --> 00:54:52,800
and then eventually we’ll just make this leap faster and faster.

686
00:54:52,800 --> 00:54:55,360
Instead of releasing a model every three months, now it’s every week,

687
00:54:55,360 --> 00:54:58,800
and then every day, and then every hour, at which point we’ve basically solved it.

688
00:54:59,360 --> 00:55:03,760
I think this is a good point as well, because you asked how far off the current

689
00:55:03,760 --> 00:55:07,280
paradigm we are from being able to do this. We’ve done a bit of research into this,

690
00:55:07,280 --> 00:55:11,280
and people have done a lot of research. At a really large scale, when you wash

691
00:55:11,280 --> 00:55:14,800
out enough noise and you have large enough batches, this outer-loop process

692
00:55:14,800 --> 00:55:18,000
of putting data into mid-training and creating our own environments does work

693
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:21,760
in some sort of continual learning regime. But the problem is, when you zoom in close

694
00:55:21,760 --> 00:55:26,240
enough at a micro level — I’ve got one model and I’m trying to update it again for a law

695
00:55:26,240 --> 00:55:30,960
firm or something, and I’m trying to do that very continuously with a relatively small amount of

696
00:55:30,960 --> 00:55:38,560
data — all the methods kind of break down a bit. If I SFT the model on just successful traces,

697
00:55:39,280 --> 00:55:44,480
off-policy or on-policy, eventually in the very iterative regime, when you’re doing

698
00:55:44,480 --> 00:55:48,720
hundreds of these micro-updates, you see catastrophic forgetting.

699
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:53,040
You see forgetting of previous information learned on top of the base model that was much earlier on,

700
00:55:53,040 --> 00:55:57,040
and you see degradation of general capabilities.

701
00:55:58,320 --> 00:56:02,080
On-policy distillation seems to push this horizon out a little bit, but it still

702
00:56:02,080 --> 00:56:09,120
eventually succumbs to the same thing. RL is good at getting capabilities in,

703
00:56:09,120 --> 00:56:13,360
but it’s not as good at getting knowledge in, this very explicit knowledge of, "Ah,

704
00:56:13,360 --> 00:56:17,440
okay, this person does this at this law firm, and this is a very specific process we find."

705
00:56:17,440 --> 00:56:19,280
You have to pour in a lot of compute to create the right

706
00:56:19,280 --> 00:56:23,600
environments to get the knowledge in with RL. Do you think the fundamental issue here — why

707
00:56:23,600 --> 00:56:28,560
you get worse at these other skills or there’s forgetting — is fundamentally an issue of capacity

708
00:56:28,560 --> 00:56:31,520
or an issue of techniques? A little bit of both.

709
00:56:31,520 --> 00:56:37,680
I think SFT and even on-policy distillation can be way too destructive.

710
00:56:38,720 --> 00:56:44,160
The reason RL is so nice is because it changes a very, very small amount about the model.

711
00:56:44,160 --> 00:56:46,400
There’s a lot of evidence for why this is the case.

712
00:56:47,040 --> 00:56:52,480
It just tweaks it in this very, very small loss valley to get it into the right point.

713
00:56:53,040 --> 00:56:57,120
But that also then limits what you can do with RL, how much you can actually change the model.

714
00:56:57,120 --> 00:57:00,000
So you’re saying the reason this isn't a winner-take-all, potentially,

715
00:57:00,000 --> 00:57:03,840
is that it is just very hard to distill that much information into the base model?

716
00:57:03,840 --> 00:57:06,480
Without ruining something, in an iterative fashion.

717
00:57:06,480 --> 00:57:08,800
It’s easy to distill it into a different base model.

718
00:57:08,800 --> 00:57:13,600
This is where I think it’s mostly technique. It’s definitely not that there isn’t capacity.

719
00:57:13,600 --> 00:57:17,120
If you had some model with all this data, and you take literally the same-size model and

720
00:57:17,120 --> 00:57:20,160
pre-train it from scratch with all of the stuff in mid-training, it will be better.

721
00:57:20,160 --> 00:57:24,720
I think that’s a lot of what’s happening today. There’s very much a bottleneck that stops us from

722
00:57:24,720 --> 00:57:28,000
just keeping training the same model forever, versus just getting all the data from the old

723
00:57:28,000 --> 00:57:31,840
model and training a new model from scratch. This is exactly as Charlie was saying:

724
00:57:31,840 --> 00:57:34,320
some combination of plasticity and catastrophic forgetting.

725
00:57:35,520 --> 00:57:39,840
If you just naively train on non-stationary data, because you’re adding new data as you go,

726
00:57:39,840 --> 00:57:43,520
this is messing with the data distribution, so the old stuff is just forgotten.

727
00:57:43,520 --> 00:57:46,320
We don’t really have good methods to stop that from happening.

728
00:57:46,320 --> 00:57:49,040
So maybe in the limit you’re just bottlenecked by retraining the model

729
00:57:49,040 --> 00:57:52,800
from scratch with all this new information. Yes, which of course is very expensive.

730
00:57:52,800 --> 00:57:55,440
Training a model from scratch is expensive. But you’re going to do that anyways.

731
00:57:56,000 --> 00:57:59,520
Not necessarily. Maybe eventually, if you have continual learning, you never train a new model.

732
00:57:59,520 --> 00:58:02,480
You just have a model and it keeps learning and expanding.

733
00:58:02,480 --> 00:58:05,680
But there might be some deep technical reason why that’s very difficult.

734
00:58:06,640 --> 00:58:11,680
That’s the question. I think we have pushed back how much from scratch we need to do.

735
00:58:11,680 --> 00:58:15,520
It is definitely possible now to take the pre-trained base and do

736
00:58:15,520 --> 00:58:20,320
very good mid-training on top of that, kind of continuously, plus some RL from different

737
00:58:20,320 --> 00:58:23,680
checkpoints that are later on in the training. That’s looking more like continual learning,

738
00:58:23,680 --> 00:58:27,600
but it’s certainly not the case of taking the most recent model, applying a couple of very small

739
00:58:27,600 --> 00:58:32,080
updates, and iteratively never losing anything. Sorry, but I’m a bit confused, because isn’t this

740
00:58:32,080 --> 00:58:35,120
literally what happens during training? During post-training or something,

741
00:58:35,120 --> 00:58:40,400
you have a model that’s already gone through so much training, and then you distill some

742
00:58:40,400 --> 00:58:44,080
fork that’s been further RL’d. Isn’t that literally what happens?

743
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:47,280
But it’s still at a large enough scale, I think, that you’re washing out a lot

744
00:58:47,280 --> 00:58:52,720
of the noise, and you’re not just focused on one distribution, which, as Beren said, is the issue.

745
00:58:53,280 --> 00:58:59,760
If you’re just focusing on one task— But in the eventual regime you’d be doing…

746
00:58:59,760 --> 00:59:05,200
There are billions of deployed instances. You’re learning from all of them at once,

747
00:59:05,200 --> 00:59:09,280
so hopefully there’s some washing out of noise from that.

748
00:59:09,280 --> 00:59:13,440
Maybe at that scale, yeah. As Charlie was saying,

749
00:59:13,440 --> 00:59:16,320
you can definitely do continual mid-training for a long time, and you can roll back to a

750
00:59:16,320 --> 00:59:18,880
checkpoint and give it new mid-training data. But at the same time,

751
00:59:18,880 --> 00:59:21,840
you can’t do this indefinitely. If you just keep continually training the

752
00:59:21,840 --> 00:59:27,760
same base forever, it asymptotes at some point. You can’t just learn new stuff in that base.

753
00:59:27,760 --> 00:59:31,040
This is why people end up training new bases. Otherwise you would just keep mid-training

754
00:59:31,040 --> 00:59:33,440
the same base forever. Whenever I finish recording

755
00:59:33,440 --> 00:59:37,440
an interview, I immediately brain-dump all my thoughts into Slack—things like what was

756
00:59:37,440 --> 00:59:40,960
most interesting and what should get cut. This ensures that my editors have all the

757
00:59:40,960 --> 00:59:45,840
context they need to start editing the episode. But these brain dumps don’t have clear timestamps,

758
00:59:45,840 --> 00:59:52,160
and my unedited recordings are many hours long. It can take a ton of editor time just to find the

759
00:59:52,160 --> 00:59:55,360
exact moments I was referencing. So we decided to try adding

760
00:59:55,360 --> 00:59:59,600
a Grok Bot producer to our chat. Now, whenever one of my editors posts

761
00:59:59,600 --> 01:00:04,480
a rough cut of an episode, Grok Bot opens the transcript on its own computer and starts working,

762
01:00:04,480 --> 01:00:08,000
usually before I’ve even seen the message. It takes the notes I dropped into Slack

763
01:00:08,000 --> 01:00:10,320
and highlights the relevant snippets in the transcript.

764
01:00:10,320 --> 01:00:13,920
It also uses a big case file I’ve compiled with all my preferences,

765
01:00:13,920 --> 01:00:17,360
so it can suggest potential edits. When it’s done, it sends me its

766
01:00:17,360 --> 01:00:20,240
top clip candidates so I can review everything from my phone.

767
01:00:20,240 --> 01:00:23,440
This has worked really well. Being able to send informal messages,

768
01:00:23,440 --> 01:00:27,280
like I’m texting my editor, and then have the transcript immediately reflect my

769
01:00:27,280 --> 01:00:34,000
preferences has just been so helpful. Try Grok Bot yourself at x.ai/bot.

770
01:00:34,000 --> 01:00:38,000
Let’s talk a bit about data now. I’m generally interested in this

771
01:00:38,000 --> 01:00:41,680
question of how much of AI progress is just explained by data progress.

772
01:00:41,680 --> 01:00:46,320
That doesn’t mean it will necessarily be hard to automate, but that's a separate question.

773
01:00:46,320 --> 01:00:53,600
Is there some data distribution which, if you trained current architectures on it, would result

774
01:00:53,600 --> 01:00:58,800
in a superintelligence that totally dominates human experts across every single field?

775
01:00:58,800 --> 01:01:02,720
Are we talking about pre-training plus post-training data, environments as well?

776
01:01:03,520 --> 01:01:07,600
I think the existence of this is obvious. It’s just whether we can create the right

777
01:01:07,600 --> 01:01:09,120
environment to get there. In the trivial case,

778
01:01:09,120 --> 01:01:12,560
we could just train it to output the Python file which trains the actual superintelligence.

779
01:01:12,560 --> 01:01:17,280
Just have that memorized in the weights. Yes, there’s probably a ladder of RL

780
01:01:17,280 --> 01:01:23,600
environments that is possible to construct such that you would get an AI researcher which is

781
01:01:23,600 --> 01:01:28,960
at least as good as a human researcher. But the effort to climb each successive

782
01:01:28,960 --> 01:01:34,720
rung grows kind of exponentially. Those are the two things you have to

783
01:01:34,720 --> 01:01:40,000
trade off against as to how fast we’re going to hit that final rung where it’s better.

784
01:01:40,800 --> 01:01:44,320
I think that’s fairly clear. We’re still relatively

785
01:01:44,320 --> 01:01:47,280
early in RL environment creation. There are a lot of asymmetries that

786
01:01:47,280 --> 01:01:51,680
we exploit in order to create good environments. One of the asymmetries which we’ve talked about

787
01:01:51,680 --> 01:01:56,800
before is that there are environments where it’s easier to go backwards than forwards.

788
01:01:56,800 --> 01:02:01,120
What I mean by that is, it’s very easy to define this complex data-generating process,

789
01:02:01,120 --> 01:02:04,560
and this is the latent variable you keep hidden from the model.

790
01:02:04,560 --> 01:02:09,440
You can generate arbitrarily complex environments, and the model has to do a lot of irreducible

791
01:02:09,440 --> 01:02:14,080
token spend and irreducible work to figure out what that data-generating process was.

792
01:02:14,080 --> 01:02:18,080
There are asymmetries in terms of injecting information from the real world.

793
01:02:18,080 --> 01:02:23,280
Anthropic finds a bug through tens of thousands of humans and LLMs combined,

794
01:02:23,280 --> 01:02:26,880
and turns that into a very, very neat environment which a single LLM could

795
01:02:26,880 --> 01:02:31,600
theoretically find within a few million tokens. There are all these asymmetries which we’re

796
01:02:31,600 --> 01:02:36,240
cherry-picking, and we’re counting on this kind of task-horizon generalization.

797
01:02:36,240 --> 01:02:41,120
But I think it’s just going to hit diminishing returns at some point, diminishing returns in

798
01:02:41,120 --> 01:02:44,640
how hard it is to create those environments in the first place, coming up with them, because

799
01:02:44,640 --> 01:02:50,320
you can’t necessarily just have these processes where it’s easier to go backwards than forwards.

800
01:02:50,320 --> 01:02:55,760
You actually have to sit down and construct something that looks like a long enough time

801
01:02:55,760 --> 01:02:59,120
horizon with humans, and it’s going to be a really complex task to create.

802
01:02:59,120 --> 01:03:01,920
Then there are also going to be the compute and time bottlenecks for the

803
01:03:01,920 --> 01:03:05,360
agent to actually do those tasks. I think you’re just going to start

804
01:03:05,360 --> 01:03:08,560
seeing this curve flatten out. I saw something about how someone

805
01:03:08,560 --> 01:03:17,520
fine-tuned the Talkie model, which is only trained on data up to 1930, on modern coding agent data.

806
01:03:18,320 --> 01:03:26,400
It did better than Claude 3 Opus on SWE-bench. So this model that has no knowledge of code

807
01:03:26,400 --> 01:03:30,000
whatsoever can be fine-tuned on a moderate amount of data and

808
01:03:31,520 --> 01:03:36,320
behave better as a coding agent than this much larger pre-trained model, which is pretty crazy.

809
01:03:36,320 --> 01:03:42,000
It kind of shows you that once you have an example of the right expert behavior,

810
01:03:42,000 --> 01:03:48,480
it’s actually surprisingly easy to copy that into a relatively weak model.

811
01:03:48,480 --> 01:03:53,280
But a counterexample to that is a paper recently where they trained a model up to

812
01:03:53,280 --> 01:03:59,600
fifth-grade maths, and also primary-school English and stuff, so it was a decent language model.

813
01:03:59,600 --> 01:04:03,360
They tried to RL it to do late high school and college maths.

814
01:04:03,360 --> 01:04:06,000
The gap was just too large. They couldn’t get it to climb at all.

815
01:04:06,560 --> 01:04:11,280
But if you did successive rungs of year 7 maths and then year 8 maths… and so on,

816
01:04:11,280 --> 01:04:15,440
you could obviously climb to year 12. Again, it’s just what is the distance

817
01:04:15,440 --> 01:04:17,840
between the rungs on those ladders, and how hard is it to create?

818
01:04:18,400 --> 01:04:22,720
This just comes back to the RL signal problem. RL is not very good at exploring right now.

819
01:04:23,520 --> 01:04:28,800
If the model can’t get it in 128 rollouts, it’s very unlikely to get signal to progress.

820
01:04:28,800 --> 01:04:33,120
This is why in RL we need curricula, whereas in pre-training we don’t, because that’s not

821
01:04:33,120 --> 01:04:37,200
a problem for pre-training at all. Again, pre-training data is

822
01:04:37,200 --> 01:04:42,320
different to post-training data. I imagine as we continue on, humans

823
01:04:42,320 --> 01:04:46,240
will be involved less and less, but that doesn’t change the fact that you’re bottlenecked on how

824
01:04:46,240 --> 01:04:51,040
much signal you can extract from the real world. There’s a lot of signal in the world,

825
01:04:51,040 --> 01:04:52,720
and that’s true. There’s people doing

826
01:04:53,280 --> 01:04:56,720
spreadsheet tasks, there’s people doing legal tasks and all this sort of stuff.

827
01:04:56,720 --> 01:05:00,560
But at the capability frontier of where the models are at now,

828
01:05:00,560 --> 01:05:06,880
how many bits in the world are actually really relevant to improving the model’s capabilities?

829
01:05:06,880 --> 01:05:09,920
How many new maths problems are being solved that are just beyond

830
01:05:09,920 --> 01:05:13,280
the reach or grasp of the current models? How many new coding problems are being

831
01:05:13,280 --> 01:05:16,080
created or solved that are beyond the reach of the current models?

832
01:05:16,080 --> 01:05:19,680
I think that’s why the diminishing returns kick in, because even the world as a whole is not

833
01:05:19,680 --> 01:05:22,480
giving you the bits, going back to the start of this, that are useful for tipping you into

834
01:05:22,480 --> 01:05:26,480
the next basin of capability. I totally agree with this.

835
01:05:26,480 --> 01:05:28,800
It’s really a question of where the signal is coming from.

836
01:05:30,960 --> 01:05:33,840
In pre-training, the signal is already in Common Crawl.

837
01:05:33,840 --> 01:05:38,400
For the tasks that you care about in pre-training, the problem is not getting signal at all.

838
01:05:38,400 --> 01:05:41,760
It’s filtering out all the noise that exists. That’s quite an automatable process.

839
01:05:41,760 --> 01:05:45,040
But as the models get better, as we enter mid-training and post-training,

840
01:05:45,040 --> 01:05:48,320
the signal just doesn’t exist anywhere in the original data we have.

841
01:05:48,320 --> 01:05:52,960
No amount of filtering will get this. There’s no hidden proof of a Millennium

842
01:05:52,960 --> 01:05:55,680
Prize problem sitting in Common Crawl that we can just filter until we see it.

843
01:05:56,560 --> 01:06:00,560
At that point, you have to get bits some other way, either from humans directly, asking them

844
01:06:00,560 --> 01:06:05,120
to write out their reasoning, or by creating environments where humans decide what environment

845
01:06:05,120 --> 01:06:10,240
should be created and what the objectives of these environments are, or some kind of training on

846
01:06:10,240 --> 01:06:13,680
the human data that exists in deployment. You have to get the bits from somewhere.

847
01:06:14,640 --> 01:06:17,760
There’s a question of how much of the progress in pre-training is being driven by data.

848
01:06:18,320 --> 01:06:24,640
I did this investigation with Jerry Han, who’s a student at Princeton, where we trained all

849
01:06:24,640 --> 01:06:31,520
the recipes from 2019 till now pairwise with all the data sets from 2019 to now.

850
01:06:31,520 --> 01:06:36,000
You’re training GPT-2 on the newest data set, like Ultra-FineWeb.

851
01:06:36,000 --> 01:06:42,320
You train Delphi, which is the newest open source training recipe, on the Pile or some old data set.

852
01:06:42,320 --> 01:06:48,000
You do the whole grid. You see, getting to some

853
01:06:48,000 --> 01:06:52,960
level of capabilities, how much less compute does it take, across this grid?

854
01:06:52,960 --> 01:06:58,000
You see that the data seems to explain something like a 12.0x compute efficiency

855
01:06:58,000 --> 01:07:01,520
gain, but the architecture improvements explain something like a 3.7x compute

856
01:07:01,520 --> 01:07:06,800
efficiency gain, at a very small scale. To the extent that that is true at large

857
01:07:06,800 --> 01:07:10,080
scale — that most of the pre-training compute efficiency gains are coming from

858
01:07:10,080 --> 01:07:16,160
better data — how much can that continue? Can you keep filtering data more and more

859
01:07:16,160 --> 01:07:20,560
and building more and more synthetic data? Do you have a sense of how much this kind of

860
01:07:20,560 --> 01:07:26,000
pre-training progress can continue? My prior is that, again, the

861
01:07:26,000 --> 01:07:31,040
low-hanging fruit is somewhat exhausted. We got the internet as this big block,

862
01:07:31,040 --> 01:07:36,240
and it’s not like the internet is necessarily growing at the same rate.

863
01:07:36,240 --> 01:07:38,000
All the useful stuff on the internet isn’t growing at the same rate.

864
01:07:38,720 --> 01:07:42,880
We’ve probably got a bunch of 0.1% loss drops to go, but definitely

865
01:07:42,880 --> 01:07:46,800
not as many as have currently occurred. But it’s also really interesting that you

866
01:07:46,800 --> 01:07:53,920
find this cumulative 33x improvement across both. I think it was Epoch or someone who estimated

867
01:07:53,920 --> 01:08:01,440
3x a year since 2019, which would imply something like 3⁷, over 2,000X improvement.

868
01:08:01,440 --> 01:08:05,200
So where’s that missing 100x or whatever coming from?

869
01:08:05,200 --> 01:08:08,720
That probably gives you a good signal of how much of this is post-training.

870
01:08:08,720 --> 01:08:14,320
I think the explanation has to be that a lot of the compute efficiency gains are scale dependent,

871
01:08:14,320 --> 01:08:20,000
and we’re starting at extremely small scale. That raises a question of whether the data compute

872
01:08:20,000 --> 01:08:25,920
efficiency gains or the algorithmic compute efficiency gains have more scale dependence.

873
01:08:25,920 --> 01:08:27,120
I don't know if you have a prior on that.

874
01:08:27,120 --> 01:08:29,040
We just didn’t have enough compute to investigate that question.

875
01:08:29,600 --> 01:08:36,320
Just naively, theoretically, the scale dependence of the architecture is fairly well known,

876
01:08:36,320 --> 01:08:40,400
and you can fit a straight line to it. Whereas I would have no idea how to

877
01:08:40,400 --> 01:08:44,640
do that for combining pre-training plus post-training data and mid-training data.

878
01:08:44,640 --> 01:08:48,320
Funnily enough, I feel like data is actually more important with scale.

879
01:08:48,320 --> 01:08:51,840
I feel like architectures are kind of a one-time thing.

880
01:08:53,360 --> 01:08:57,600
Saying just an X% efficiency gain is kind of misleading, because what an architecture does is

881
01:08:57,600 --> 01:09:01,440
let you reach a qualitatively new regime which you couldn’t reach with the old architecture.

882
01:09:01,440 --> 01:09:04,640
Within that regime, obviously the data is the primary thing determining it.

883
01:09:04,640 --> 01:09:10,400
But if we didn’t have even GQA, if we were doing full attention all day, it would be

884
01:09:10,400 --> 01:09:15,040
ridiculously expensive to do a million context. Because of that, we could never use the data

885
01:09:15,040 --> 01:09:18,400
which is actually at a million context, so we couldn’t get these capabilities.

886
01:09:18,400 --> 01:09:23,120
Even though if you just do a naive "how much does this do at 2K context", where the architecture

887
01:09:23,120 --> 01:09:27,120
isn’t unlocking anything, then the data will look much more important than in some sense it is.

888
01:09:27,920 --> 01:09:32,560
It’s unclear to me that these things are really just multiplicative gains in this way.

889
01:09:32,560 --> 01:09:36,480
I see. So what’s your take on the scale dependence of data?

890
01:09:37,280 --> 01:09:41,920
On scale dependence, I think a lot of the mid-training and post-training data

891
01:09:41,920 --> 01:09:46,480
we have now actually gets better with scale, because a lot of it — the very long context

892
01:09:46,480 --> 01:09:51,440
horizon environment stuff — really requires big models to be able to make use of it.

893
01:09:51,440 --> 01:09:54,800
If you try and train your 100 million parameter model on SWE-bench traces,

894
01:09:54,800 --> 01:09:57,440
it’s not going to get anywhere. It’s not going to show you the

895
01:09:57,440 --> 01:10:00,800
same kind of improvement that you would get if you train an actual sensible size model on it.

896
01:10:01,680 --> 01:10:05,200
It’s hard as well now because so many of the architecture changes — you look at Kimi,

897
01:10:05,200 --> 01:10:10,800
for instance, or DeepSeek — they’re doing these architectural modifications not just with dropping

898
01:10:10,800 --> 01:10:14,560
the pre-training loss in mind, but with how the models are going to be used in the real world.

899
01:10:15,120 --> 01:10:19,120
The inference efficiency, having some form of compressed attention in the DeepSeek models,

900
01:10:19,120 --> 01:10:23,280
is not necessarily geared around a fundamental trade-off improvement.

901
01:10:23,280 --> 01:10:26,000
It’s just, "Okay, we’re considering how the models are going to be used."

902
01:10:27,040 --> 01:10:28,800
One question I’m curious about, to understand the future,

903
01:10:28,800 --> 01:10:34,400
is how parameter scaling will go as we’re getting into more of an RL-heavy regime.

904
01:10:37,440 --> 01:10:42,240
You can look at open source architectures and see how fast parameters have been scaling.

905
01:10:42,240 --> 01:10:46,400
Maybe it’s roughly 2x every year for frontier open source models.

906
01:10:46,400 --> 01:10:52,080
To the extent that even frontier closed source models have 100B or 200B active parameters,

907
01:10:52,080 --> 01:10:55,360
do you think that keeps 2x-ing year over year? Or, now that we’re in an RL regime where you

908
01:10:55,360 --> 01:11:01,760
also want to conserve compute on rollouts… Also, maybe there is a threshold effect where you have

909
01:11:01,760 --> 01:11:06,240
enough capacity and at that point increasing parameters arbitrarily doesn’t matter as much.

910
01:11:06,240 --> 01:11:10,560
Do you guys have a sense of, in 2030, how many active parameters a frontier model will have?

911
01:11:11,120 --> 01:11:17,200
I think for the next few years, because we are so focused on doing longer and longer horizon

912
01:11:17,200 --> 01:11:23,280
rollouts for RL, where inference efficiency matters a lot, it feels like the models aren’t

913
01:11:23,280 --> 01:11:27,600
necessarily saturated on their ability to do that. The bottleneck is still the environments.

914
01:11:27,600 --> 01:11:33,280
So we might see a little bit of a plateau. I have a feeling that Mythos and the GPT

915
01:11:33,280 --> 01:11:37,600
models are much smaller than the 10 trillion parameter range that people are talking about.

916
01:11:38,240 --> 01:11:42,320
Even just naively comparing them to open source models, you can probably back out that conclusion.

917
01:11:44,000 --> 01:11:47,760
Probably for the next few years, I wouldn’t imagine a huge growth in the number of parameters.

918
01:11:47,760 --> 01:11:51,360
But again, there’s so many different things to trade off here.

919
01:11:51,360 --> 01:11:55,280
You decide the size of your model based on how much pre-training data you have,

920
01:11:55,280 --> 01:11:58,960
and then the difficulty of the RL environments that you’ve got to train on.

921
01:11:58,960 --> 01:12:04,560
You ideally want to get to the optimal point where you can get a decent pass@1 or something

922
01:12:04,560 --> 01:12:08,480
on the hardest environments you have. It wouldn’t make sense to make a bigger

923
01:12:08,480 --> 01:12:12,880
model pass there, because then you’re just paying much more inference than you need to.

924
01:12:12,880 --> 01:12:18,960
So a lot of it depends on how quickly Mercor and the in-house teams can scale up the complexity

925
01:12:18,960 --> 01:12:22,640
of the RL environments they’re training on. I would expect the models to keep getting

926
01:12:22,640 --> 01:12:26,720
bigger just because people are scaling up compute and the GPUs are getting bigger.

927
01:12:26,720 --> 01:12:33,040
But exactly how much they get bigger depends a bit on the scaling laws in non-obvious ways.

928
01:12:34,000 --> 01:12:40,640
One thing is that I think data efficiency is going to be a bigger driver than compute efficiency of

929
01:12:40,640 --> 01:12:46,800
the exact architectures people use, now that we’re getting to the regime where we’re running

930
01:12:46,800 --> 01:12:51,920
low on high quality pre-training data. That might affect how sparse you want

931
01:12:51,920 --> 01:12:54,800
to make the model. I also think we don’t

932
01:12:55,680 --> 01:12:59,920
understand sparsity that well. Parameters are a different

933
01:12:59,920 --> 01:13:08,160
resource than active parameters. Sparsity has definitely increased a bit,

934
01:13:08,160 --> 01:13:11,360
but it’s not clear that it’s going to keep increasing without bound.

935
01:13:11,360 --> 01:13:16,080
There might be some kind of sweet spot. There’s an argument that sparsity should

936
01:13:16,080 --> 01:13:21,200
make data efficiency worse, because you might have to learn the same thing on multiple experts,

937
01:13:22,880 --> 01:13:27,280
though that’s debatable. I don’t think we have a

938
01:13:27,280 --> 01:13:32,480
good enough theory of scaling laws that we really understand why sparsity is helping,

939
01:13:33,440 --> 01:13:38,880
how much it’ll help, and if that’ll plateau at some point at a certain level of sparsity.

940
01:13:38,880 --> 01:13:42,000
Sorry, can you spell out exactly what the implication of data

941
01:13:42,000 --> 01:13:46,080
efficiency would be on parameters? It sounds like you’d say there

942
01:13:46,080 --> 01:13:49,360
should be less sparsity, but what are the other implications on parameter scaling?

943
01:13:50,240 --> 01:13:56,400
Just that with the scaling law, you’re not trying to optimize compute efficiency.

944
01:13:56,400 --> 01:13:59,680
You have all your choices you can make on the architecture.

945
01:14:00,240 --> 01:14:04,480
Each of these gives you a different scaling law. Traditionally, you would look at some

946
01:14:04,480 --> 01:14:08,320
kind of envelope based on compute. You would look at performance versus

947
01:14:08,320 --> 01:14:20,120
compute and take the envelope of the best models. But if we’re making that decision based on data —

948
01:14:20,720 --> 01:14:33,600
we’re assuming we can spend a lot of compute, so data is on our x-axis instead of compute — then

949
01:14:33,600 --> 01:14:38,960
we just get a different set of optima, or a different set of models that are on that frontier.

950
01:14:39,760 --> 01:14:44,160
I also don’t think that we’ve necessarily doubled the size of the

951
01:14:44,160 --> 01:14:49,440
models every year for the last few years. People have been training 1 trillion

952
01:14:49,440 --> 01:14:53,040
parameter models for at least a few years. There was even an open source one called Falcon.

953
01:14:53,040 --> 01:14:56,000
Liam from Periodic Labs, I think, posted yesterday on Twitter about how

954
01:14:56,000 --> 01:15:00,720
an early experiment was training a 1 trillion parameter model that was very, very sparse.

955
01:15:00,720 --> 01:15:05,120
That was what they did before OpenAI, at Google, the Switch Transformer.

956
01:15:05,920 --> 01:15:11,680
It was very, very good at knowledge but terrible at reasoning because it was so sparse.

957
01:15:12,720 --> 01:15:18,080
It feels like we’ve been playing in this 100 billion up to 2 trillion parameter range for

958
01:15:18,080 --> 01:15:19,680
at least a little bit. It certainly hasn’t

959
01:15:19,680 --> 01:15:23,600
been this nice linear increase. I feel like there’s two things.

960
01:15:23,600 --> 01:15:27,920
As Charlie was saying, inference efficiency is super important for RL rollouts.

961
01:15:27,920 --> 01:15:31,680
This will really push down active parameters quite a lot.

962
01:15:31,680 --> 01:15:35,280
I think the total parameters really depends a lot on the hardware as well.

963
01:15:35,280 --> 01:15:39,760
You really need very high memory bandwidth and VRAM size to actually be able to serve

964
01:15:39,760 --> 01:15:43,600
multi-trillion parameter models. Right now, people are still using

965
01:15:43,600 --> 01:15:46,240
a lot of H100s and stuff. As everyone moves to GBs and

966
01:15:46,240 --> 01:15:51,280
then Vera Rubins, we’ll get more of the ability to scale and actually serve and

967
01:15:51,280 --> 01:15:56,080
do large RL inference at larger scales. The data question I think is interesting,

968
01:15:56,080 --> 01:16:00,960
because naively, larger models are much more sample efficient in the actual data points.

969
01:16:00,960 --> 01:16:04,080
Even if you’re not saturating the model, it’s still better to go bigger,

970
01:16:04,080 --> 01:16:08,960
because larger models generalize better and get to a better loss for the same amount of data.

971
01:16:09,520 --> 01:16:13,440
Right now I think we have a lot of data, and that’s not the constraint.

972
01:16:13,440 --> 01:16:16,720
Compute is. So we’re having smaller models which are very inference efficient.

973
01:16:16,720 --> 01:16:19,600
But if compute is no longer the bottleneck, it might come back to larger models which are

974
01:16:20,800 --> 01:16:25,360
undersaturated, but have this generalization ability because they’re much larger.

975
01:16:25,360 --> 01:16:31,680
If you just look at the basic Chinchilla scaling law and you just maximize out

976
01:16:31,680 --> 01:16:36,080
parameters, it actually decreases the amount of data you need to get to the same loss very little.

977
01:16:36,720 --> 01:16:41,360
If you go to infinity on parameters, the amount of data you need I think goes down less than 10X,

978
01:16:41,360 --> 01:16:45,200
just because of the nature of the power law. But we’re now on the way-too-much-data side

979
01:16:45,200 --> 01:16:47,920
of the Chinchilla laws. Right now we over-train

980
01:16:47,920 --> 01:16:49,920
models according to Chinchilla. So we could easily get back to

981
01:16:49,920 --> 01:16:53,840
a point where, as we’re running out of data, we move back to the Chinchilla optimal point,

982
01:16:53,840 --> 01:16:59,360
or even a bit on the under-training model side. But surely, even with these new chips that come

983
01:16:59,360 --> 01:17:02,800
online, we’re just going to be so compute bottlenecked for the next few years that

984
01:17:02,800 --> 01:17:06,240
that won’t necessarily be the case. This depends on the ratio you have of

985
01:17:06,240 --> 01:17:09,600
training and inference compute, really. If you’re super bottlenecked on data,

986
01:17:09,600 --> 01:17:12,320
not on compute, you should go bigger. If you’re super bottlenecked on compute,

987
01:17:12,320 --> 01:17:16,480
you should always go smaller. You can also use computer-generated

988
01:17:16,480 --> 01:17:20,160
synthetic data, so it’s one of these very hard things to predict.

989
01:17:20,160 --> 01:17:24,400
I think part of the reason it took people so long to figure out the scaling laws in the first place

990
01:17:24,400 --> 01:17:28,880
was that if you don’t get all these things right, then you don’t get such a clean relationship.

991
01:17:30,880 --> 01:17:37,200
The beautiful straight lines on graphs hide a lot of complexity in how you have to make sure

992
01:17:37,200 --> 01:17:42,320
to scale every hyperparameter the right way, or parameterize your optimizer in a way that

993
01:17:43,280 --> 01:17:47,360
scales and where you don’t have to change your hyperparameters as you change the model size.

994
01:17:47,360 --> 01:17:53,040
Bugs have their own clean scaling laws as well. Like with Kaplan forgetting the cosine

995
01:17:53,040 --> 01:17:56,480
annealing thing, or even just not considering embedding parameters, I think.

996
01:17:56,480 --> 01:18:00,080
That messed up the estimate at smaller models because embedding

997
01:18:00,080 --> 01:18:08,320
parameters are a decent size of the model. A bit on RL. A year ago, a lot of people

998
01:18:08,320 --> 01:18:14,560
were making this argument that RL will not be super successful at scaling for models.

999
01:18:14,560 --> 01:18:19,200
John, you wrote a research paper where you were pointing out that models learn one

1000
01:18:19,200 --> 01:18:22,480
bit per episode when you RL. They learn, "Did I get the

1001
01:18:22,480 --> 01:18:25,920
answer right or did I get it wrong?" Then I wrote some blog posts earlier

1002
01:18:25,920 --> 01:18:28,720
this year where I was like, "It’s even worse than that," because when the pass rate is low and the

1003
01:18:28,720 --> 01:18:35,040
model is very unlikely to get the answer right, it learns almost nothing at all from an RL episode.

1004
01:18:35,040 --> 01:18:39,200
But I look at the models today, and they seem pretty smart.

1005
01:18:39,200 --> 01:18:43,600
It seems to be the result of scaling up RL. Beren, you had a post a few weeks ago where

1006
01:18:43,600 --> 01:18:47,920
you were trying to explain what’s going on. Why has RL been more successful than

1007
01:18:47,920 --> 01:18:52,720
one would have naively thought? I think the success of RL comes

1008
01:18:52,720 --> 01:18:56,560
down to a bunch of different things. First, what is slightly underestimated

1009
01:18:56,560 --> 01:18:59,120
is the mid-training. An awful lot of what we

1010
01:18:59,120 --> 01:19:03,680
see as successes of RL actually comes from very, very good mid-training data, which is where we’re

1011
01:19:04,240 --> 01:19:08,480
essentially doing pre-training but on synthetic reasoning data and the kind of environments

1012
01:19:08,480 --> 01:19:12,960
that get the model warm-started for RL. This takes the model almost 80% of the

1013
01:19:12,960 --> 01:19:16,880
way to the final RL checkpoint often. Then what RL does on top of that is

1014
01:19:18,960 --> 01:19:22,240
essentially tweaking the policy. This is one of the reasons why it doesn’t

1015
01:19:22,240 --> 01:19:25,600
need as many bits as you would naively think. It doesn’t have to learn all of these

1016
01:19:25,600 --> 01:19:28,080
behaviors from scratch. It needs just a few bits

1017
01:19:28,080 --> 01:19:31,680
from these episodes, which you do get. The other thing that I point out in my

1018
01:19:31,680 --> 01:19:36,560
blog is that these bits are extremely high signal compared to regular pre-training,

1019
01:19:36,560 --> 01:19:40,960
which is why you need RL at all versus just SFT-ing on successful reasoning traces.

1020
01:19:40,960 --> 01:19:44,320
Because it’s exactly the bits about how to get the answer right.

1021
01:19:44,320 --> 01:19:47,440
There’s two things. Yes, one, it’s exactly the bits about how to get the answer right.

1022
01:19:47,440 --> 01:19:52,400
But this is not exactly how you think of it, because in SFT, you have a trace.

1023
01:19:52,400 --> 01:19:55,040
You have, say, a bunch of math reasoning and then the answer at the end.

1024
01:19:55,040 --> 01:19:58,080
The bit is still there. You still SFT on the answer token.

1025
01:19:58,960 --> 01:20:01,840
What’s important is that the objective ignores all the other bits.

1026
01:20:01,840 --> 01:20:07,360
In SFT, you have to try and match the exact reasoning tokens that the model produces.

1027
01:20:07,360 --> 01:20:10,400
You’re essentially getting too many bits about the exact way this other

1028
01:20:10,400 --> 01:20:14,080
model you’re training on reasons. For RL, you only get the one bit.

1029
01:20:14,080 --> 01:20:19,120
That means that signal is not drowned out in the noise of all the other bits the model has.

1030
01:20:20,400 --> 01:20:24,000
It’s really a super dramatic increase in the signal-to-noise ratio during training,

1031
01:20:24,000 --> 01:20:28,080
which is why RL is so dramatically efficient in terms of steps.

1032
01:20:31,440 --> 01:20:36,960
There’s been so much debate about what RL does to the model versus mid-training or SFT or whatever.

1033
01:20:38,400 --> 01:20:42,240
Everyone talks about how pass@1 will go up, but pass@256 will go down.

1034
01:20:42,240 --> 01:20:46,560
Very rare correct reasoning traces will be down-weighted and outweighed

1035
01:20:46,560 --> 01:20:53,360
by a gradient signal from easier reasoning traces. I think the simple way to view RL now is that if

1036
01:20:53,360 --> 01:20:58,000
you have a large enough amount of compute to sample a large enough group size — such

1037
01:20:58,000 --> 01:21:03,680
that your probability of getting a bunch of correct answers is past some not insignificant

1038
01:21:03,680 --> 01:21:10,880
probability — then it will be up-weighted. To Beren’s point, mid-training and more

1039
01:21:10,880 --> 01:21:15,280
pre-training — the pass@1, the starting point for RL — scales in

1040
01:21:15,280 --> 01:21:18,640
a log number of pre-training tokens. Can I ask some very basic questions?

1041
01:21:20,320 --> 01:21:24,400
That answer makes sense, and maybe there’s empirical research which

1042
01:21:24,400 --> 01:21:28,800
shows that this is what’s happening. But then I just look at the models

1043
01:21:28,800 --> 01:21:34,320
themselves… I don’t know what’s happened. Maybe you can give me a sense of what is

1044
01:21:34,320 --> 01:21:44,240
the basis of the AI progress over the last year. Maybe it’s just up-weighting the policies which

1045
01:21:44,240 --> 01:21:48,240
were going to do the correct thinking anyways. But it just seems like qualitatively,

1046
01:21:48,240 --> 01:21:56,480
the models have gotten so much more capable. Maybe there’s no inherent contradiction there.

1047
01:21:56,480 --> 01:22:02,000
But how do we square the relatively small impact this take would imply that

1048
01:22:02,000 --> 01:22:06,560
RL would have with the actual qualitative capabilities the models seem to be gaining?

1049
01:22:06,560 --> 01:22:08,400
One thing I want to point out here is that it doesn’t

1050
01:22:08,400 --> 01:22:13,120
necessarily imply that RL has a small effect. Even if you have a few bits and you only change

1051
01:22:13,120 --> 01:22:18,059
the parameters a small amount, the actual impact on function space — the input-to-output mapping

1052
01:22:18,059 --> 01:22:22,880
the model learns — can still be super dramatic. Even one bit can change your function space a lot.

1053
01:22:22,880 --> 01:22:25,920
It can rule out half the hypothesis space, which is huge.

1054
01:22:25,920 --> 01:22:29,680
I don’t think it’s necessarily the case that small amounts of bits, small amounts of RL, once you’re

1055
01:22:29,680 --> 01:22:33,440
starting from a really good point, means that you don’t have dramatic impacts in behavior.

1056
01:22:33,440 --> 01:22:36,880
At least… not necessarily. I think it comes down to two things.

1057
01:22:36,880 --> 01:22:41,120
The first thing is that everyone was hoping that RL would generalize this reasoning

1058
01:22:41,120 --> 01:22:44,080
across all these different domains. I don’t think we necessarily got

1059
01:22:44,080 --> 01:22:47,840
this horizontal generalization. Just training on math doesn’t

1060
01:22:47,840 --> 01:22:51,920
necessarily make you the greatest coder. You do have to do RL on code environments.

1061
01:22:51,920 --> 01:22:55,840
I think what we did get, though, is horizon generalization.

1062
01:22:55,840 --> 01:23:00,160
The models just learned how to use more tokens for longer and still

1063
01:23:00,160 --> 01:23:04,400
make progress on some sort of task. You can train on environments where

1064
01:23:04,400 --> 01:23:07,440
they get longer and longer and then put them into a completely new environment.

1065
01:23:07,440 --> 01:23:10,800
Yes, they may not have generalized the reasoning patterns which allow them to do well in that

1066
01:23:10,800 --> 01:23:14,560
environment, but they’ve at least generalized the ability to continue on that task for longer,

1067
01:23:14,560 --> 01:23:18,400
which is correlated with success. There was a paper called EdgeBench which

1068
01:23:18,400 --> 01:23:23,440
showed that the rate at which models can work for longer is doubling every three months.

1069
01:23:23,440 --> 01:23:27,280
That’s clear evidence of generalization. The final way to think about it is,

1070
01:23:28,480 --> 01:23:34,720
in pre-training, there’s this idea of quanta. You have this very smooth pre-training loss curve.

1071
01:23:34,720 --> 01:23:39,120
When you look at what’s happening in the model, the model is learning all these very discrete

1072
01:23:39,120 --> 01:23:43,520
tasks, and there’s all these emergent points where there’s a phase transition.

1073
01:23:43,520 --> 01:23:46,000
It didn’t have induction heads, now it has induction heads.

1074
01:23:46,000 --> 01:23:50,080
There’s tens of thousands, millions, probably hundreds of millions of these things.

1075
01:23:50,080 --> 01:23:52,960
You average them all together and you get this very smooth loss curve.

1076
01:23:54,000 --> 01:23:58,960
To an extent, a similar thing is happening for RL. There is this very slow outer loop,

1077
01:23:58,960 --> 01:24:01,760
as Beren mentioned. We will train a model and then

1078
01:24:01,760 --> 01:24:07,280
RL it, and then in the next model iteration of training, we will dump a bunch of these synthetic

1079
01:24:07,280 --> 01:24:11,040
reasoning traces into the mid-training data. We’re kind of hitting all these quanta for

1080
01:24:11,040 --> 01:24:15,840
all these different tasks, and on an individual task level, it may look like a phase transition.

1081
01:24:15,840 --> 01:24:20,720
You’re suddenly going from a 0.5% pass rate to a 90% pass rate on a particular

1082
01:24:21,360 --> 01:24:24,880
finance task or Excel task or whatever. But you average all these things together,

1083
01:24:24,880 --> 01:24:30,560
plus the horizon generalization, and you kind of go, "Wow, we’ve got qualitatively better models."

1084
01:24:30,560 --> 01:24:34,560
I think a lot of this as well is just… RL does generalize a bit.

1085
01:24:34,560 --> 01:24:38,560
You get some transfer between math and code, or puzzles and math and this kind of stuff.

1086
01:24:38,560 --> 01:24:43,040
Also, the sheer amount of environments people are targeting is just vastly greater.

1087
01:24:43,760 --> 01:24:48,320
Before, when you tried to do some task which you do in your daily life, two years ago,

1088
01:24:48,320 --> 01:24:50,960
the labs wouldn’t really care about this. They wouldn’t train the model for it.

1089
01:24:50,960 --> 01:24:53,360
Now it’s just so much broader. They have a lot of environments

1090
01:24:53,360 --> 01:24:56,320
targeting this specific thing. Earlier in the conversation we were talking

1091
01:24:56,320 --> 01:25:02,400
about RL in the context of causing this entropy collapse, or just concentrating probability on

1092
01:25:02,400 --> 01:25:08,000
solutions the base model had already done, and causing relatively sparse updates in the policy.

1093
01:25:08,000 --> 01:25:16,560
But I think there’s also another story about RL, which is going back to the

1094
01:25:16,560 --> 01:25:21,600
Atari games and then AlphaGo coming up with move 37, the super creative move.

1095
01:25:21,600 --> 01:25:25,280
Because it was never initialized on human data, it can think in ways that

1096
01:25:25,280 --> 01:25:29,040
humans are not even thinking and come up with extremely creative solutions.

1097
01:25:29,600 --> 01:25:35,120
Do you have a sense of when we should expect, or if we should expect, RL on LLMs to result

1098
01:25:35,120 --> 01:25:41,800
in things like move 37, extreme creativity even beyond human creativity, because there’s just de

1099
01:25:41,800 --> 01:25:47,120
novo initialization of intelligence? A couple of things here.

1100
01:25:47,120 --> 01:25:52,400
First off, I think that AlphaGo is using MCTS, which obviously does more exploration and stuff

1101
01:25:52,400 --> 01:25:56,560
than regular policy gradients. But I also think that RL doesn’t

1102
01:25:56,560 --> 01:26:01,760
necessarily reduce the creativity. This is obviously qualitative, but if

1103
01:26:01,760 --> 01:26:06,560
we look at the OpenAI-Hugging Face incident, these models were coming up with multiple zero-days at

1104
01:26:06,560 --> 01:26:11,600
a time to break out of the sandbox. This is clearly some level of move 37

1105
01:26:11,600 --> 01:26:16,720
creativity already, which we just get from the general generalization properties of the LLMs.

1106
01:26:17,280 --> 01:26:22,960
It’s definitely not the case that RL is totally destroying entropy, especially on long horizons.

1107
01:26:23,600 --> 01:26:28,080
One thing that people call creativity is just solving hard search problems.

1108
01:26:31,680 --> 01:26:36,000
Move 37 is obviously an example of that, or writing some kind of poem

1109
01:26:36,000 --> 01:26:42,000
that satisfies a ton of different constraints. That’s something AI is obviously going to be

1110
01:26:42,000 --> 01:26:48,160
extremely good at, if trained for it. Then there’s another way in which the

1111
01:26:51,120 --> 01:26:56,880
diversity of the models’ outputs is a lot lower after RL, and they develop these tics.

1112
01:26:58,480 --> 01:27:02,000
Even though the models seem like they’re good at writing, when you do some kind of

1113
01:27:02,000 --> 01:27:10,160
distributional analysis, you find that they’re reusing certain themes all the time and they’re

1114
01:27:10,160 --> 01:27:15,840
using the same character names all the time. You’re not getting the same kind of diversity

1115
01:27:15,840 --> 01:27:21,520
that you get from human authors. You’re getting one really good style.

1116
01:27:21,520 --> 01:27:28,400
So I think that kind of diversity has definitely been cut down by RL a lot.

1117
01:27:29,120 --> 01:27:38,000
In fact, since we were talking about distillation earlier, one thing that’s happening is that so

1118
01:27:38,000 --> 01:27:44,240
many people are distilling, mostly from Claude, that all the open-weight models write the same

1119
01:27:44,240 --> 01:27:50,240
way as Claude and have the same tics. This seems kind of concerning to me,

1120
01:27:50,240 --> 01:27:56,320
that we’re having this monoculture emerge. Again, I don’t think this is fundamental

1121
01:27:56,320 --> 01:27:59,760
to RL as a method, though. The same with distillation. Even with

1122
01:27:59,760 --> 01:28:03,840
distillation, you’re just training on the data. Just because your data is not super broad,

1123
01:28:03,840 --> 01:28:06,560
that doesn’t mean the training method itself is somehow wrong.

1124
01:28:06,560 --> 01:28:08,480
It’s a problem with the data. I think a lot of the RL entropy collapse, for

1125
01:28:08,480 --> 01:28:15,440
instance, is basically due to exploitation of fairly simple verifiers when you don’t have a

1126
01:28:15,440 --> 01:28:17,680
huge diversity of environments. The writing, for instance,

1127
01:28:19,200 --> 01:28:22,240
is presumably graded by some judge. The judge has some specific tics,

1128
01:28:22,240 --> 01:28:26,000
and the model is learning to reward hack the judge, and that’s why it collapses.

1129
01:28:26,000 --> 01:28:30,960
But this is really a problem with the judge. It’s not a problem with RL in general.

1130
01:28:30,960 --> 01:28:34,720
Okay, super rapid-fire predictions about the future.

